// ============================ SECTION 1 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Predictive Analytics for Suicide Risk',
  order: 1,
  description: 'EHR-based risk models, machine-learning flags, digital phenotyping, and crisis-line detection',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '1', title: 'Predictive Analytics for Suicide Risk', subtitle: 'How EHR Models, Machine Learning, and Digital Signals Are Used to Flag Risk', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>Why Prediction Entered the Suicide-Prevention Conversation</h2><p>For most of the history of clinical practice, the estimation of suicide risk was an entirely human act. A clinician sat with a person, asked about thoughts of death, listened for hopelessness, weighed protective factors against warning signs, and arrived at a judgment that was documented, acted upon, and revisited. That judgment remains the irreducible core of suicide-risk assessment and nothing in this course displaces it. What has changed over the past fifteen years is that large health systems, technology companies, and crisis-response organizations have begun to layer a second source of signal on top of clinical judgment: statistical models that scan structured data and assign each person a numerical estimate of suicide risk. These models do not interview anyone. They do not perceive affect, notice a flat tone of voice, or register the pause before a person answers a hard question. They consume data that already exist in electronic health records, on digital platforms, or in the text of a crisis conversation, and they output a probability or a flag{{callout:risk-flag}}. Understanding what those outputs are, where they come from, and what they can and cannot do is now part of competent practice for clinicians who work anywhere near suicide risk.</p><p>The motivation behind predictive analytics for suicide is sobering and worth stating plainly. Suicide is a leading cause of death, and despite decades of research, clinicians' ability to predict who will die by suicide and when has remained close to chance when measured against the rarity of the outcome. A landmark meta-analysis by Franklin and colleagues in 2017, synthesizing fifty years of longitudinal studies, found that the predictive accuracy of individual risk factors for suicidal thoughts and behaviors was only marginally better than chance and had not meaningfully improved across the half-century of research reviewed. That finding was not an indictment of clinicians; it was a description of a genuinely hard problem. Single risk factors, considered one at a time, simply do not carry enough information to identify the small number of people who will go on to attempt or die. The hope behind machine learning is that combining hundreds or thousands of weak signals, learned from the records of millions of people, might extract a predictive signal that no single factor and no unaided human can see.</p><p>That hope is real, but it is bounded, and the bound is the subject of the next section. For now, the task is to understand the landscape of systems that are actually in operation. These are not hypothetical. They run today, inside the Veterans Health Administration, inside large integrated health systems, inside crisis lines, and inside the consumer platforms that billions of people use daily. A clinician who encounters one of their outputs, whether a flag in a chart or an alert from a crisis-line vendor, needs a working mental model of what produced it.</p></div>`, callouts: { 'risk-flag': { label: 'Risk Flag', type: 'clinical', body: 'An algorithmic output indicating a person belongs to a statistically elevated-risk group over a defined time window — an invitation to assess, not a clinical verdict of intent.' } } },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Electronic Health Record Models: REACH VET and the Mental Health Research Network</h3><p>The most mature operational example of suicide-risk prediction in the United States is the Veterans Health Administration's REACH VET program — Recovery Engagement and Coordination for Health, Veterans Enhanced Treatment. REACH VET applies a predictive statistical model to the electronic health records of every veteran enrolled in VA care. Each month the model scores the entire population on a set of variables drawn from the record: demographic information, prior mental health and substance-use diagnoses, prior suicide attempts, recent inpatient or emergency utilization, medication fills, and other administrative signals. The model then identifies the roughly highest-risk fraction of a percent of patients. Those individuals are flagged, and a designated coordinator at their facility is notified to reach out, review the treatment plan, and re-engage the veteran in care. McCarthy and colleagues described the development and rationale of this model, and subsequent evaluation found that veterans identified by REACH VET had measurably more outpatient engagement and fewer crisis episodes after the outreach than they would have been expected to otherwise. Importantly, REACH VET is designed as a care-coordination prompt, not an alarm — the flag triggers a human clinician to look, think, and reach out, not an automatic intervention.</p><p>A parallel body of work comes from the Mental Health Research Network, a consortium of large integrated health systems including Kaiser Permanente regions and HealthPartners. Researchers in this network, including Kessler, Simon, and colleagues, developed prediction models using the rich combined data of these systems: structured diagnoses, prior self-harm, prescription records, and crucially, responses to routine depression questionnaires such as the PHQ-9{{callout:phq-9}}, including the ninth item that asks directly about thoughts of being better off dead or of self-harm. These models predicted suicide attempts and suicide deaths in the days and months following an outpatient visit substantially better than clinicians' unaided judgment or single screening items. The MHRN models illustrate a key design principle: the predictive lift came from combining many ordinary data elements, none of which was decisive alone, into a single calibrated estimate.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'imageText', order: 4, title: 'From Population Scan to Clinical Outreach', content: `<div class="cr-prose"><p>EHR-based models such as REACH VET operate at the population level. Rather than waiting for a person in crisis to present, the model scans the whole enrolled population on a recurring schedule and surfaces a small high-risk group for proactive human follow-up. This inverts the traditional reactive posture of crisis care. The clinical value is not the score itself but the conversation it prompts: a coordinator who calls a veteran who has not been seen in months, reviews their safety plan{{callout:safety-plan}}, and reconnects them with care. The model is a way of allocating scarce clinical attention toward people who would otherwise go unnoticed until a crisis, not a substitute for the assessment that the outreach clinician still performs.</p></div>`, image: '', imageAlt: 'Diagram of a population-level risk model surfacing a small high-risk group for clinician outreach', imagePosition: 'right', order: 4 },
    { type: 'text', order: 5, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Machine-Learning Risk Flags and How They Differ from Checklists</h3><p>It is worth distinguishing the machine-learning models described here from the structured checklists and screening instruments clinicians already know. A traditional risk-stratification tool assigns points for a fixed set of factors — prior attempt, current plan, access to means, recent loss — and sums them into a category. The thresholds and weights are set by expert consensus and remain fixed. A machine-learning model, by contrast, learns its weights from data. It is shown the records of a very large population along with the known outcomes — who attempted, who did not — and an algorithm adjusts the contribution of each variable to maximize predictive accuracy on that historical data. The result can incorporate hundreds or thousands of variables and can capture interactions that no human would hand-specify: perhaps the combination of a particular diagnosis, a recent medication change, and a missed appointment carries risk that none of those three alone would suggest.</p><p>This data-driven flexibility is the source of both the promise and the peril of these systems. The promise is sensitivity to patterns invisible to checklists. The peril is that the model learns whatever is in the data, including the artifacts, gaps, and inequities of how care has historically been delivered and recorded. A model trained on records in which a particular group was under-assessed or under-documented may systematically under- or over-estimate their risk. A model that learns that frequent health-system contact predicts a recorded attempt may simply be detecting who has access to care, not who is most at risk. These are not reasons to reject the tools; they are reasons to interpret their outputs with the same critical posture a clinician brings to any instrument whose construction they did not personally control.</p><p>Clinicians should also understand the temporal structure of these predictions. Most operational models produce a risk estimate for a defined window — for example, the probability of an attempt within ninety days. A flag is therefore a statement about elevated probability over a period, not a prediction that something will happen today. This distinction matters enormously for how the flag should be used. It justifies proactive engagement and monitoring; it does not by itself justify an emergency response, and treating every flag as an imminent-danger signal is one of the predictable failure modes addressed later in this course.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 6, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Social Media, Digital Phenotyping, and Crisis-Line Detection</h3><p>Beyond the clinical record, a second stream of suicide-risk signal comes from digital behavior. Coppersmith and colleagues pioneered the application of natural language processing to social-media text, demonstrating that linguistic patterns — shifts in pronoun use, references to pain or hopelessness, changes in posting time and frequency, and the affective tone of language — carry statistical information about suicidal ideation and risk. This line of research gave rise to the broader concept of digital phenotyping: the moment-to-moment quantification of a person's behavior and physiology using data from the devices they already use. Smartphone metadata such as sleep-correlated usage patterns, movement, social-communication frequency, and typing dynamics can be modeled as proxies for the mood, sleep disruption, and withdrawal that clinicians watch for in person.</p><p>Major technology platforms have deployed crisis-detection systems built on these signals. Search engines and social platforms detect queries and posts indicative of suicidal intent and surface crisis resources, including the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline in the United States, and in some cases route content to human reviewers or, in defined circumstances, to emergency services. Crisis text and chat lines apply machine-learning triage to the live text of conversations, scoring messages to help route the highest-acuity contacts to counselors faster. These systems extend detection far beyond the walls of the clinic, into the everyday digital environment where many people in distress first express it.</p><p>The clinical and ethical questions these systems raise are substantial and are taken up in Section 3. People who post or search did not consent to clinical risk assessment; the populations represented online are not the populations seen in clinics; and the consequences of a false positive in this setting — an unwanted welfare check, a loss of privacy — can be serious. For now, the essential point is descriptive: suicide-risk detection no longer lives only in the assessment interview. It is embedded in records, in phones, and in platforms, and clinicians increasingly encounter its outputs as part of the information surrounding a client.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 65, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Why These Systems Emerged Now</h3><p>It is worth pausing to understand why suicide-risk prediction has become operational in the last decade and not before, because the answer shapes how a clinician should regard the resulting tools. Three developments converged. First, electronic health records became near-universal, creating, for the first time, large, longitudinal, machine-readable datasets that link clinical histories to outcomes. A model cannot learn to predict suicide attempts without thousands of records in which attempts are documented alongside the preceding clinical data; only the maturation of the electronic record made such datasets possible at scale. Second, computational methods for learning from high-dimensional data — the family of techniques loosely called machine learning — matured to the point where they could be applied to clinical data routinely rather than only in specialized research settings. Third, and most importantly, the field confronted the sobering evidence, crystallized by the Franklin meta-analysis, that fifty years of risk-factor research had not produced clinically useful prediction. That evidence created both the motivation to try something new and the humility to recognize that the new approach, too, would have to prove itself rather than be assumed superior.</p><p>These origins matter because they explain both the promise and the limits of the tools. The promise is that combining many data elements, learned from large populations, can extract predictive signal that single factors and unaided clinicians cannot. The limit is that the model can only learn what is in the data, and the data reflect the imperfect, uneven, and sometimes inequitable history of how care has been delivered and recorded. A clinician who understands this origin story will neither dismiss these tools as hype nor accept them as oracles. They will regard them as what they are: empirically derived instruments, built from imperfect data, that may usefully concentrate clinical attention if interpreted with appropriate humility.</p><p>There is also a sociological dimension worth naming. Health systems face genuine pressure to prevent suicide among the people in their care, and predictive analytics offer something that feels like a systematic, scalable answer. That appeal is legitimate, but it can also generate a kind of automation bias — a tendency to trust a system simply because it is computational and produces a confident-looking number. The antidote to automation bias is exactly the statistical and clinical literacy this course builds: knowing what the number means, what it does not mean, and what the human clinician must still do. A flag that arrives wrapped in the authority of an algorithm deserves the same critical scrutiny a clinician would apply to any instrument, perhaps more, precisely because its apparent authority can lull the unwary into deference.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 66, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Data Behind a Flag</h3><p>To interpret a flag, it helps to know concretely what kinds of data feed these models. Structured diagnoses are a primary input: histories of depression, bipolar disorder, substance-use disorders, post-traumatic stress, and prior self-harm all carry statistical weight. Utilization patterns matter too: recent emergency-department visits, psychiatric hospitalizations, and — perhaps counterintuitively — missed or canceled appointments can signal disengagement that models associate with elevated risk. Pharmacy data contribute: certain medication classes, recent changes in prescriptions, and gaps in fills are all observable in the record and learnable by a model. Screening responses, especially the ninth item of the PHQ-9 that asks directly about thoughts of death or self-harm, add a self-reported signal that improves prediction when it is available. Demographic and social variables — age, sex, marital status, and in some models indicators of social circumstance — round out the typical feature set.</p><p>What is striking about this list is how ordinary each element is. None of these variables is, by itself, a strong predictor; the Franklin meta-analysis demonstrated as much for single factors. The predictive value emerges only from their combination, and from the model's ability to weight and interact them in ways that reflect the patterns in a very large training population. This is the central insight of the machine-learning approach: many weak signals, properly combined, can carry information that no single signal does. But it is also the source of the approach's opacity. Because the model's prediction reflects a complex weighting of hundreds of variables, no clinician can look at a flag and reconstruct why it fired. The flag is, in this sense, a summary judgment whose reasoning is not transparent — which is one more reason it must prompt human assessment rather than substitute for it.</p><p>Clinicians should also appreciate what is absent from the data. The model does not know what happened in the person's life last week unless it generated a record. It does not know the person's reasons for living, the quality of their relationships, the meaning they make of their distress, or the protective web of connection and purpose that clinical assessment uncovers. It does not perceive the person at all. Everything that makes suicide-risk assessment a human and relational act lies outside the model's view. This is not a defect to be corrected by better data; it is a categorical limit. The richest, most decision-relevant information about a person's risk is precisely the information that only an encounter can provide, and the model's silence about it is a permanent feature of what these tools are.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 7, calloutType: 'clinical', title: 'A Flag Is an Invitation to Assess', content: `<p>Whatever its source — an EHR model, a digital signal, or a crisis-line triage score — an algorithmic suicide-risk flag is best understood as an invitation to conduct or update a clinical assessment, not as the assessment itself. The score tells you that a person belongs to a statistically elevated group over a defined time window. It does not tell you their current intent, their access to support, their reasons for living, or what they need right now. Only a clinical encounter can establish those, and only that encounter, documented and acted upon, constitutes suicide-risk assessment in any clinical or legal sense.</p>` },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 8, title: 'Operational Suicide-Risk Systems at a Glance', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'REACH VET (Veterans Health Administration)', content: 'A population-level EHR model that scores every enrolled veteran monthly, identifies the highest-risk fraction, and assigns a coordinator to conduct proactive outreach, review the safety plan, and re-engage the veteran in care. Designed as a care-coordination prompt rather than an alarm.' },
      { title: 'Mental Health Research Network models', content: 'Prediction models developed across large integrated health systems (including Kaiser Permanente regions and HealthPartners) using diagnoses, prior self-harm, prescription data, and PHQ-9 responses to estimate the probability of suicide attempt or death following a visit. Demonstrated predictive lift over clinician judgment and single screening items.' },
      { title: 'Social-media and digital-phenotyping detection', content: 'Natural-language-processing and behavioral models applied to platform posts, searches, and smartphone-derived signals to detect expressions of suicidal ideation and surface crisis resources such as 988. Pioneered in research by Coppersmith and colleagues and deployed at scale by major technology companies.' },
      { title: 'Crisis-line and chatbot triage', content: 'Machine-learning systems that score the live text of crisis chats and texts to help route the highest-acuity contacts to counselors more quickly and to flag conversations that may require escalation. Augments, but does not replace, the trained crisis counselor.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'videoEmbed', order: 9, videoTitle: 'How Predictive Suicide-Risk Models Work', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/PLACEHOLDER_suiciderisk', description: 'An overview of how electronic-health-record and machine-learning models generate population-level suicide-risk estimates and how clinicians use those flags to prompt outreach and assessment.' },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 10, question: 'Which statement best characterizes the REACH VET program as it operates within the Veterans Health Administration?', options: [
      { text: 'It automatically initiates an emergency response for any veteran whose score crosses a threshold.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'It scores the enrolled population monthly and flags the highest-risk fraction for clinician outreach and care re-engagement.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'It replaces the clinical suicide-risk assessment for veterans it identifies.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'It applies only to veterans who self-refer to a crisis line.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'REACH VET applies a predictive model to the full enrolled population each month, surfaces a small highest-risk group, and assigns a coordinator to conduct proactive outreach and re-engage the veteran in care. It is a care-coordination prompt, not an automatic emergency response, and it does not replace clinical assessment.' },
    { type: 'multiSelect', order: 11, question: 'Which of the following are genuine data sources that operational suicide-risk prediction models have drawn upon? (Select all that apply)', options: [
      { text: 'Structured diagnoses and prior self-harm history in the electronic health record', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Responses to routine depression questionnaires such as the PHQ-9', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Linguistic patterns in social-media text', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'A clinician\'s certified prediction of the exact date of a future attempt', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Smartphone-derived behavioral signals used in digital phenotyping', isCorrect: true }
    ], explanation: 'EHR diagnoses and self-harm history, PHQ-9 responses, social-media language, and smartphone behavioral signals are all real inputs to operational or research models. No model relies on a clinician certifying an exact future date; suicide cannot be predicted with that precision.' },
    { type: 'matching', order: 12, matchingInstructions: 'Match each suicide-risk detection approach to its defining feature.', matchingPairs: [
      { term: 'REACH VET', definition: 'Monthly population scan of veterans\' records that flags the highest-risk fraction for outreach' },
      { term: 'MHRN models', definition: 'Health-system prediction using diagnoses, prescriptions, and PHQ-9 to estimate post-visit risk' },
      { term: 'Digital phenotyping', definition: 'Quantifying behavior from smartphone and device data as proxies for mood and withdrawal' },
      { term: 'NLP on social media', definition: 'Detecting linguistic markers of distress and ideation in posted text' }
    ] },
    { type: 'flashcardDeck', order: 13, instructions: 'Review these foundational terms in suicide-risk prediction.', flashcards: [
      { id: 'f1', front: 'Predictive analytics (suicide risk)', back: 'The use of statistical or machine-learning models applied to existing data to estimate the probability of a future suicidal outcome over a defined window.' },
      { id: 'f2', front: 'REACH VET', back: 'The VA program that scores all enrolled veterans monthly and flags the highest-risk fraction for proactive clinician outreach and care re-engagement.' },
      { id: 'f3', front: 'Digital phenotyping', back: 'Moment-to-moment quantification of behavior and physiology from a person\'s own devices, used as proxies for clinically relevant states such as mood, sleep, and social withdrawal.' },
      { id: 'f4', front: 'Risk flag', back: 'An algorithmic output indicating that a person belongs to a statistically elevated-risk group; an invitation to assess, not a clinical determination of intent.' },
      { id: 'f5', front: 'Prediction window', back: 'The defined time period (e.g., 90 days) over which a model estimates the probability of an outcome; a flag describes elevated probability across that window, not imminence today.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 14, question: 'Think about your own practice setting. Where, if anywhere, do algorithmic suicide-risk signals already enter your information stream — through an EHR flag, a crisis-line vendor, or a platform alert? How do you currently make sense of those signals, and what would you want to understand better about where they come from?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 15, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'Suicide-risk prediction has moved from single-factor checklists, which perform near chance (Franklin et al., 2017), toward machine-learning models that combine many weak signals learned from large populations.',
      'REACH VET and Mental Health Research Network models are mature operational examples that scan populations and flag a small high-risk group to prompt human outreach, not to replace clinical assessment.',
      'Digital phenotyping and natural-language processing extend detection beyond the clinic into social media, smartphones, and crisis lines.',
      'Every algorithmic flag is an invitation to assess over a defined window, not a verdict about current intent or imminence.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ SECTION 2 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Performance and Its Limits',
  order: 2,
  description: 'Sensitivity, specificity, PPV, the low-base-rate problem, calibration, and alert fatigue',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '2', title: 'Performance and Its Limits', subtitle: 'Why a High AUC Still Produces Many False Positives for a Rare Event', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>The Vocabulary of Prediction</h2><p>To use an algorithmic risk flag responsibly, a clinician must be able to interpret the numbers that describe how well the model performs. This is not a detour into statistics for its own sake; it is the difference between trusting a flag appropriately and either over-reacting to a meaningless signal or dismissing a meaningful one. The key terms are sensitivity, specificity, and positive predictive value, and the most important lesson of this entire course may be the relationship among them when the outcome being predicted is rare.</p><p>Sensitivity is the proportion of people who will actually have the outcome — here, a suicide attempt or death — whom the model correctly flags. A model with ninety percent sensitivity catches nine of every ten people who go on to attempt. Specificity is the proportion of people who will not have the outcome whom the model correctly does not flag. A model with ninety percent specificity correctly clears nine of every ten people who are not going to attempt. Both of these sound reassuringly high, and a model achieving both would be described, in the language of model evaluation, as having a high area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, or AUC — a single summary number, ranging from 0.5 (chance) to 1.0 (perfect), that captures the model's ability to discriminate cases from non-cases across all thresholds. Suicide-prediction models in the literature have reported AUCs in the high 0.7s to high 0.8s, which by the conventions of predictive modeling is genuinely good discrimination.</p><p>And yet a model with excellent sensitivity, excellent specificity, and a strong AUC can still be wrong the overwhelming majority of the time when it raises a flag. Understanding why is the single most consequential piece of statistical literacy for clinicians working near these tools, and it is the subject of the low-base-rate problem.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Low-Base-Rate Problem, Worked Through</h3><p>Suicide is, statistically, a rare event. Even among people receiving mental health care, the proportion who will die by suicide in a given year is a small fraction of one percent, and even suicide attempts over a defined window remain uncommon relative to the size of any clinical population. This rarity — the low base rate — is what makes prediction so hard, and it is not a flaw that better algorithms can engineer away. It is a mathematical feature of the problem.</p><p>Consider a worked example. Imagine a population of one hundred thousand patients, and suppose that over the prediction window one hundred of them — a base rate of one in a thousand — will attempt suicide. Now apply an excellent model with ninety percent sensitivity and ninety-five percent specificity. The ninety percent sensitivity means the model correctly flags ninety of the one hundred true cases. So far, so good. But the ninety-five percent specificity means that of the 99,900 people who will not attempt, the model incorrectly flags five percent of them — that is, 4,995 people. Now look at everyone the model flagged: ninety true cases plus 4,995 false alarms, for a total of 5,085 flags. The positive predictive value — the probability that a flagged person actually has the outcome — is ninety divided by 5,085, which is under two percent. In other words, even with a model most researchers would call excellent, more than ninety-eight of every hundred flags are false positives.</p><p>This is not a contrived result; it is the structural reality of predicting any rare event, and it has been demonstrated empirically for suicide. Belsher and colleagues, in a 2019 systematic review published in JAMA Psychiatry, examined the predictive performance of suicide risk models across the literature and concluded that despite reasonable discrimination, the models produced positive predictive values that were uniformly low — too low, the authors argued, to support their use as standalone clinical decision tools at the point of care. The review did not say the models were worthless; it said their outputs must be understood for what they are: low-precision signals that identify enriched-risk groups, not individualized verdicts.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 4, calloutType: 'warning', title: 'High AUC Does Not Mean High Precision', content: `<p>A clinician who hears that a model "has an AUC of 0.85" or "is ninety percent sensitive" may reasonably assume that a flag from that model is usually correct. For a rare outcome like suicide, that assumption is false. Because the people who will not attempt vastly outnumber those who will, even a small false-positive rate generates far more false alarms than true cases. The result is a positive predictive value that can be under two percent. Belsher et al. (2019) found uniformly low positive predictive values across suicide-prediction models and cautioned against treating their outputs as standalone clinical decisions.</p>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 5, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Calibration: A Second, Distinct Question</h3><p>Discrimination — captured by sensitivity, specificity, and AUC — answers the question of whether the model can separate higher-risk from lower-risk people. Calibration answers a different question: when the model says a person has, say, a five percent probability of an attempt, do five percent of such people actually attempt? A model can discriminate well yet be poorly calibrated, systematically over- or under-stating absolute risk. Calibration matters clinically because the absolute probability is what should inform proportionate response. A poorly calibrated model that inflates probabilities will drive over-response and alarm; one that deflates them will breed false reassurance. Good evaluations report calibration alongside discrimination, and clinicians reviewing any deployed tool should ask whether its calibration was assessed in a population like theirs. A model calibrated on one health system's data may be miscalibrated when applied to a different population with a different base rate or different documentation practices.</p><p>Calibration also drifts over time. A model trained on data from one era may degrade as clinical practice, coding, populations, and even the meaning of recorded variables change. This phenomenon, sometimes called dataset shift, means that a tool validated years ago is not guaranteed to perform as advertised today. Responsible deployment includes ongoing monitoring of performance, not a one-time validation followed by indefinite trust.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 6, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Alert Fatigue and the Human Cost of False Positives</h3><p>The low positive predictive value of suicide-risk models is not merely a statistical curiosity; it has direct consequences for the clinicians who receive the alerts and for the people who are flagged. When the great majority of flags do not correspond to imminent risk, clinicians quickly learn, consciously or not, that flags are usually false. This is alert fatigue, a well-documented phenomenon across clinical decision support: when a system cries wolf often enough, its warnings are tuned out, and the rare true signal is lost in the noise of false ones. Alert fatigue is dangerous precisely because it can lead to the dismissal of the very cases the system exists to catch.</p><p>There is also a cost borne by the flagged person. A false positive is not free. Depending on the workflow, it may mean an unexpected and unwanted contact, a more restrictive level of observation, an entry in a record that follows the person, increased scrutiny, or in the consumer-technology context, a welfare check that the person did not request and may experience as intrusive or even traumatizing. When ninety-eight of a hundred flags are false, these costs are imposed overwhelmingly on people who were not going to attempt. This is why the design of any risk-flag workflow must weigh not only the benefit of catching true cases but the aggregate harm of the false positives it generates — a balance that is ethical as much as it is statistical, and one taken up directly in the next section.</p><p>None of this means risk models should be abandoned. It means their outputs must be matched to proportionate, low-harm responses. A flag that triggers a gentle outreach and a safety-plan review imposes modest cost on the many false positives while still benefiting the few true cases. A flag that triggers an involuntary response imposes severe cost on a large majority who did not need it. The appropriate use of a low-precision signal is to allocate attention and care, not to authorize coercion.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 67, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Threshold Choices and the Sensitivity-Specificity Trade-off</h3><p>One reason model performance can feel slippery is that a single model does not have one sensitivity and one specificity; it has a whole curve of them, and where on that curve the model operates is a design choice. Every predictive model produces a continuous risk score, and a threshold must be chosen above which a person is flagged. Set the threshold low, and the model flags more people: sensitivity rises (fewer true cases missed) but specificity falls (more false positives). Set the threshold high, and the model flags fewer people: specificity rises (fewer false alarms) but sensitivity falls (more true cases missed). This trade-off is unavoidable and is captured by the receiver operating characteristic curve, which plots sensitivity against the false-positive rate across all possible thresholds. The area under that curve, the AUC, summarizes discrimination across all thresholds, but it does not tell you which threshold a deployed system actually uses — and that choice has enormous practical consequences.</p><p>The threshold choice is not a purely technical decision; it is a values decision dressed in statistics. Choosing a low threshold prioritizes catching true cases at the cost of many false alarms and the burdens those impose. Choosing a high threshold prioritizes minimizing false alarms at the cost of missing true cases. There is no statistically correct answer, because the answer depends on how the consequences of false positives and false negatives are weighed — a judgment that belongs to clinicians, ethicists, and the people affected, not to the algorithm. Programs such as REACH VET set their threshold deliberately to flag only a very small fraction of the population, accepting that they will miss some true cases in exchange for keeping the flagged group small enough that meaningful human outreach is feasible. A clinician encountering a flag should understand that the threshold producing it embodies someone's value judgment about this trade-off, and that the judgment may or may not match the clinician's own setting and resources.</p><p>This framing also clarifies why importing a model from one context to another is risky. A threshold calibrated to flag the top fraction of a percent in a large national system may, in a small clinic with a different base rate and different resources, flag either too many people to follow up meaningfully or too few to be worth the effort. Performance numbers reported for one deployment do not automatically transfer. The responsible question is always: at the threshold this system actually uses, in a population like mine, how many people will be flagged, how many of those will be true cases, and can my setting respond proportionately to that volume?</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 68, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The False Negative and the Limits of Reassurance</h3><p>Much of this section has emphasized false positives, because their sheer volume for a rare outcome is the most counterintuitive and consequential feature of these models. But the false negative deserves equal moral attention, and in some ways it is the more dangerous error. A false negative is a person who will attempt or die but whom the model does not flag. Because no model is perfectly sensitive, false negatives are inevitable, and they carry a specific clinical danger: the risk that a clinician will read the absence of a flag as evidence of safety. This is a grave misuse of the tool. The absence of a flag means only that the person did not cross the model's threshold given the data the model could see. It does not mean the person is safe, and it certainly does not relieve the clinician of the duty to assess risk through ordinary clinical means.</p><p>This is one of the most important practical warnings in the entire course. A risk model can, if misused, generate false reassurance — a clinician sees no flag and relaxes their vigilance, when in fact the model simply lacked the information or the sensitivity to detect a real and present risk. The person may be in crisis for reasons entirely outside the data the model consumed: an acute loss, a relationship rupture, a precipitating event that no record captured. The model is blind to all of it. A clinician who allows the absence of an algorithmic flag to lower their clinical suspicion has inverted the proper relationship between the tool and the assessment. The clinical assessment is primary and continuous; the flag, when present, is a supplementary prompt; and the absence of a flag is not a clinical finding at all.</p><p>The discipline that follows is straightforward: never let an algorithm — by its silence or its noise — replace the clinician's own active inquiry into risk. Ask the questions you would ask regardless of any flag. Listen for what the data cannot capture. Treat a flag as a reason to look harder, and treat the absence of a flag as no reason at all to look less. The model is a supplement to vigilance, never a substitute for it, and its most dangerous failure mode in everyday practice is the false comfort of a screen with no alerts on it.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'imageText', order: 7, title: 'Reading a Confusion Matrix for a Rare Outcome', content: `<div class="cr-prose"><p>A confusion matrix lays out four cells: true positives (flagged and attempted), false positives (flagged but did not attempt), false negatives (not flagged but attempted), and true negatives (not flagged and did not attempt). For a rare outcome, the false-positive cell dominates the flagged column even when the model is good, which is why positive predictive value is low. When you review a model's reported performance, look past the headline AUC and ask for the numbers behind these four cells in a population the size of yours. That single exercise reveals how many false alarms each true case will be accompanied by — the information you actually need to design a proportionate response.</p></div>`, image: '', imageAlt: 'A two-by-two confusion matrix highlighting the dominance of false positives for a rare outcome', imagePosition: 'left', order: 7 },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 8, title: 'Performance Metrics Defined', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'Sensitivity (recall)', content: 'The proportion of true cases the model correctly flags. High sensitivity means few people who will attempt are missed, but says nothing about how many false alarms accompany them.' },
      { title: 'Specificity', content: 'The proportion of non-cases the model correctly does not flag. Even 95% specificity leaves a 5% false-positive rate, which for a large non-case population produces many false alarms.' },
      { title: 'Positive predictive value (PPV / precision)', content: 'The probability that a flagged person actually has the outcome. For rare events this is often very low (under 2% in worked examples), regardless of strong sensitivity and specificity.' },
      { title: 'AUC (area under the ROC curve)', content: 'A single summary of discrimination from 0.5 (chance) to 1.0 (perfect). Suicide models reach the high 0.7s–0.8s, which is good discrimination but does not guarantee high precision.' },
      { title: 'Calibration', content: 'Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies. A model can discriminate well yet be miscalibrated, over- or under-stating absolute risk; calibration can also drift over time.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'text', order: 69, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Communicating Uncertainty to Stakeholders</h3><p>A practical challenge that follows from the statistics of this section is communication: how does a clinician explain to administrators, colleagues, or clients what a risk model can and cannot do, when the intuitive understanding of these tools is so often wrong? The instinct of many stakeholders, encountering a model described as ninety percent sensitive with a strong AUC, is to imagine that a flag means a person is very likely to attempt. The clinician who understands the low-base-rate problem has a responsibility to correct this misimpression gently but clearly, because decisions made on the false belief that flags are usually accurate will be poorly calibrated and potentially harmful. A useful way to communicate the reality is through the concrete worked example: in a population of a hundred thousand with this base rate, this excellent model flags about five thousand people, of whom roughly ninety will actually attempt. That single sentence conveys, better than any abstract statistic, that a flag identifies an enriched-risk group rather than a near-certain case.</p><p>This communication matters because the appropriate use of a low-precision tool depends on everyone in the workflow understanding its precision. An administrator who believes flags are usually accurate may design a protocol that mandates an intrusive response to every flag, imposing the over-reaction failure mode at scale. A clinician who shares the misimpression may over-respond to individual flags. And a client told that an algorithm has identified them as high-risk, without context, may experience needless alarm and stigma. Honest communication of uncertainty — neither inflating the model's precision nor dismissing its genuine, if limited, value — is part of the responsible stewardship of these tools, and it falls in significant part to the clinicians who understand the statistics to provide it.</p><p>Communicating uncertainty also includes being honest about what the field does not yet know. The performance of these models in real-world deployment, over time, across diverse populations, remains an area of active investigation, and a posture of appropriate humility — using the tools as supplements while watching their performance and remaining ready to revise — is more defensible than either uncritical adoption or reflexive rejection. The clinician who can hold and convey this nuanced stance, neither over-promising nor dismissing, models the kind of evidence-based, intellectually honest engagement with new technology that the field needs.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 9, question: 'A suicide-risk model has 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity. Applied to a population of 100,000 patients with a true attempt base rate of 1 in 1,000, why is the positive predictive value under 2%?', options: [
      { text: 'Because the model\'s AUC is below 0.5, indicating worse-than-chance discrimination.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Because the 5% false-positive rate applied to ~99,900 non-cases produces ~4,995 false alarms, vastly outnumbering the ~90 true cases caught.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Because sensitivity and specificity cannot both exceed 80% for any real model.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Because suicide attempts cannot be recorded in electronic health records.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'With a low base rate, the many non-cases generate a large absolute number of false positives even at 95% specificity. Here ~4,995 false alarms swamp the ~90 true cases caught, driving PPV under 2%. This is the structural low-base-rate problem, not a defect unique to one model.' },
    { type: 'fillInBlank', order: 10, title: 'Complete the Performance Statements', blanks: [
      { prompt: 'The metric describing the probability that a flagged person actually has the outcome is the positive ____ value.', answer: 'predictive', acceptAlternates: ['Predictive'] },
      { prompt: 'The structural reason rare-event prediction yields many false positives is called the low-____ problem.', answer: 'base-rate', acceptAlternates: ['base rate', 'baserate', 'base-rate'] },
      { prompt: 'When clinicians tune out warnings because most are false, the phenomenon is called alert ____.', answer: 'fatigue', acceptAlternates: ['Fatigue'] },
      { prompt: 'Whether a model\'s predicted probabilities match observed frequencies is a question of ____.', answer: 'calibration', acceptAlternates: ['Calibration'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'sequencing', order: 11, instructions: 'Order the steps of computing positive predictive value from a model\'s performance, from first to last.', steps: [
      { id: 's1', text: 'Identify the base rate of the outcome in the target population.', order: 1 },
      { id: 's2', text: 'Apply sensitivity to the true cases to count true positives.', order: 2 },
      { id: 's3', text: 'Apply the false-positive rate (1 minus specificity) to the non-cases to count false positives.', order: 3 },
      { id: 's4', text: 'Divide true positives by the total flagged (true positives plus false positives) to obtain PPV.', order: 4 }
    ], explanation: 'PPV depends on base rate, not just sensitivity and specificity. You must count both true and false positives given the base rate, then take true positives as a share of all positives. This is why two models with identical sensitivity and specificity can have very different PPVs in populations with different base rates.' },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 12, question: 'Imagine a vendor presents you a suicide-risk tool and emphasizes its AUC of 0.84. What questions would you now ask before relying on its flags in your setting, and how would you explain the low-base-rate problem to a colleague who is impressed by that number?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 13, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'Sensitivity and specificity describe discrimination; positive predictive value describes how often a flag is correct — and for rare events PPV is often under 2% even with strong models.',
      'The low-base-rate problem is mathematical, not a defect of a particular algorithm; it cannot be engineered away.',
      'Belsher et al. (2019) found uniformly low PPVs across suicide-prediction models and cautioned against standalone point-of-care use.',
      'Calibration is distinct from discrimination and can drift over time; alert fatigue and the real costs of false positives must shape how flags are used.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ SECTION 3 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Ethics and Law',
  order: 3,
  description: 'Duty to protect, privacy of risk flags, consent, stigma, mandated response, documentation, and equity',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '3', title: 'Ethics and Law', subtitle: 'Duty to Protect, Privacy, Consent, Equity, and the Documentation of Algorithmic Risk', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>When an Algorithm Knows Something About Your Client</h2><p>The arrival of algorithmic suicide-risk flags into clinical and consumer settings creates ethical and legal questions that have no clean precedent. Traditional suicide-risk ethics developed around a clinician who learns of risk through direct assessment and must then decide what protective action is warranted. An algorithmic flag inverts this: the clinician may be told that a model considers a person high-risk before, or even without, any clinical contact establishing that risk. What duties does that flag create? Who may see it? Did the person consent to being scored? And does the model treat all groups fairly? These questions sit at the intersection of professional ethics codes, mental-health and privacy law, and the emerging governance of artificial intelligence, and clinicians need a framework for thinking through them even though the law in many jurisdictions has not caught up.</p><p>Begin with the duty to protect. In many jurisdictions, clinicians have a legal duty, rooted in the Tarasoff{{callout:duty-to-warn}} line of cases and codified in varying ways by state, to take reasonable protective action when they determine that a client poses a serious danger. That duty has historically been triggered by clinical judgment of serious, often imminent, danger. An algorithmic flag is not a clinical judgment of imminent danger; as the previous section established, it is a low-precision statement of elevated probability over a window. The key ethical and legal principle is that a flag does not, by itself, create or discharge a duty to protect. It may prompt the assessment that leads to a clinical determination, and that clinical determination may trigger duties — but the flag is upstream of the duty, not equivalent to it. A clinician who treats every flag as a duty-triggering event will over-respond and may impose coercive interventions on people who do not need them; a clinician who ignores a flag entirely forfeits information that could inform reasonable care. The defensible middle is to treat the flag as a reason to assess and document.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Privacy of Risk Flags and Scores</h3><p>A suicide-risk score is among the most sensitive pieces of information that can exist about a person. It is a prediction about their potential for self-harm, and its disclosure could affect their employment, their relationships, their insurance, their custody of children, and their standing in a community. Within a covered health system in the United States, such a score generated from clinical data is protected health information under HIPAA and must be handled accordingly — limited to those with a treatment, payment, or operations need, secured, and not disclosed without authorization except as law permits. But the privacy questions extend beyond HIPAA. Who within an organization should be able to see a flag? Should a flag follow a person across settings? How long is it retained? A risk score that lingers in a record can stigmatize a person long after the period it described, shaping how every future clinician perceives them. Thoughtful governance limits access to those who need it for care, defines retention, and guards against the flag becoming a permanent label.</p><p>The privacy stakes are sharper still for scores generated outside the health system — by social-media platforms, search engines, or device-based monitoring. These scores are typically not protected health information at all, because the entities generating them are not health-care providers. A platform that infers suicidal intent from a person's posts holds that inference under a privacy regime quite different from clinical confidentiality, and the routing of such an inference to emergency services raises acute questions about consent, accuracy, and the potential for harm. Clinicians who receive information derived from such sources should understand its provenance and its uncertain accuracy before acting on it.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 4, calloutType: 'ethics', title: 'Consent and the Monitoring of Digital Life', content: `<p>People who post on social media, enter search queries, or carry smartphones generally did not consent to suicide-risk assessment. Detection systems that scan this activity operate, at best, on broad terms-of-service agreements that few read and that bear little resemblance to informed consent for a clinical assessment with potential coercive consequences. When such systems route a person to emergency services, the person is subjected to an intervention they never agreed to and whose accuracy is low. Clinicians and organizations that integrate any externally derived risk signal into care should be transparent with clients about what is monitored and why, and should treat consent — not a buried terms-of-service clause — as the ethical standard.</p>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 5, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Stigma, Mandated Response, and the Risk of Over-Reaction</h3><p>Embedded in the duty-to-protect and privacy discussions is the pervasive risk of stigma. A suicide-risk flag, especially a false-positive one, can mark a person in ways that follow them. If a flag triggers a more restrictive level of care, a notation that other providers read, or a mandated reporting cascade, the person experiences consequences disproportionate to a statistical signal that was, most likely, wrong. Mandated organizational responses — protocols that require a specific action whenever a flag appears — must therefore be designed with the low precision of the signal in mind. A protocol that mandates a coercive or highly intrusive response to every flag will, by the arithmetic of the previous section, impose that response overwhelmingly on people who were not at imminent risk, generating harm and eroding trust in the very systems meant to help.</p><p>This is not an argument against mandated response altogether; structure and consistency protect both clients and clinicians. It is an argument for proportionality. A mandated response of "review the safety plan and offer outreach" scales appropriately to a low-precision flag. A mandated response of "initiate involuntary evaluation" does not. The ethical design principle is that the intrusiveness of the response should match the certainty and severity that the signal actually conveys, and an algorithmic flag conveys neither certainty nor severity on its own.</p><p>There is a parallel danger of under-reaction, and it deserves equal attention. A clinician who, having absorbed the lesson that flags are usually false, dismisses every flag without assessment forfeits the genuine information the model carries. The model did identify an enriched-risk group; the true cases are concentrated among the flagged. The ethical posture avoids both poles: it neither over-reacts with coercion nor dismisses without inquiry. It uses the flag to prompt a competent assessment and lets that assessment, not the algorithm, determine the response.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 6, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Equity in Risk Scoring</h3><p>Perhaps the most serious ethical concern with algorithmic risk scoring is equity. A model learns from historical data, and historical data encode the inequities of how care has been delivered, accessed, and documented. If a group has historically been under-assessed, under-diagnosed, or under-treated, a model may learn to under-flag them, leaving real risk unaddressed. If another group has been over-surveilled, a model may over-flag them, subjecting them to disproportionate scrutiny and intrusive responses. Either error reproduces and amplifies existing disparities under a veneer of algorithmic objectivity. Worse, because the model's reasoning is opaque, these disparities can operate invisibly, unnoticed by clinicians who assume the score is neutral.</p><p>Responsible governance of any deployed suicide-risk model includes evaluating its performance separately across demographic groups — checking whether sensitivity, specificity, and calibration hold across race, ethnicity, sex, age, language, and other relevant dimensions. A model that performs well overall but poorly for a subgroup is not equitable, and deploying it without that knowledge risks systematically failing the very people who already face barriers to care. Clinicians are not usually the ones who validate models for equity, but they are entitled to ask whether such evaluation was done, and they should weight a flag's credibility accordingly. The principle is straightforward: an algorithm is not fair simply because it is an algorithm, and the burden is on those who deploy it to demonstrate that its benefits and harms are distributed justly.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 67, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Documentation: What the Record Must and Must Not Say</h3><p>Documentation sits at the intersection of clinical care, ethics, and law, and the arrival of algorithmic flags complicates it in specific ways that clinicians must navigate carefully. The governing principle is that the record should document the clinician's reasoning and the clinical encounter, not the clinician's deference to an algorithm. When a flag prompts an assessment, the defensible record notes that a flag was received, that it prompted a structured clinical assessment, what that assessment found, and what response those findings warranted. The record's center of gravity is the clinical judgment, with the flag appearing as one input that initiated the inquiry. This framing protects the clinician and serves the client, because it reflects what actually happened: a human evaluated a human and made a reasoned decision.</p><p>By contrast, a record that says, in effect, "the algorithm flagged this patient, so I did X" is troubling on multiple grounds. It suggests the clinician substituted the model's output for their own judgment, which is both clinically inappropriate and, given the low precision of the signal, indefensible. It also creates a perverse documentation trail in which the algorithm appears to be making clinical decisions. The clinician should never let the record imply that the flag, rather than the assessment, drove the response. The flag prompted; the clinician assessed; the assessment determined the action. That sequence is what the record should reflect.</p><p>There is a related concern about how a flag, once documented, propagates. A risk score or flag entered into a record may be seen by every subsequent clinician who opens the chart, shaping their perception of the person before they have formed their own. This is the stigma concern made concrete in the documentation. Thoughtful practice considers whether and how a flag should persist in the record, distinguishes clearly between the algorithmic flag and the clinical assessment it prompted, and ensures that what endures in the record is the reasoned clinical formulation rather than a bare algorithmic label that could prejudice future care. The clinician documenting today is, in effect, shaping how every future provider will see this person, and that responsibility argues for documenting clinical reasoning rather than algorithmic alarms.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 68, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Transparency, Governance, and the Clinician's Standing to Ask</h3><p>A recurring theme across these ethical touchpoints is opacity. Machine-learning models often cannot fully explain why they flagged a particular person, and the proprietary nature of many commercial tools compounds the problem: a vendor may not disclose how a model was built, what population it was trained on, what threshold it uses, or whether it was evaluated for equity. This opacity is itself an ethical problem, because clinicians are being asked to act on outputs whose provenance and validity they cannot independently verify. The principle of beneficence — acting for the client's good — and the principle of nonmaleficence — avoiding harm — both require some basis for trusting a tool, and that basis is undermined when the tool is a black box.</p><p>Clinicians are not powerless in the face of this opacity, and an important professional message of this course is that they have standing to ask hard questions. Before relying on a deployed model, a clinician or their organization may reasonably ask: What population was this model trained and validated on? Does it resemble my patients? What is its calibration, not just its discrimination? Was it evaluated for performance across demographic groups, and what did that evaluation show? What threshold does it use, and how many people does it flag? What is the defined human response when it flags someone, and who is accountable? These are not obstructionist questions; they are the due diligence that responsible practice requires when a tool will influence consequential decisions about people's safety. An organization that cannot answer them has not adequately vetted the tool it is deploying.</p><p>Governance, finally, is a shared responsibility. The individual clinician cannot single-handedly ensure that a deployed model is fair, well-calibrated, and proportionately wired into workflow. But clinicians collectively, through their participation in the committees and quality processes that govern clinical tools, can insist that these questions be answered and that detection systems be monitored over time. The ethical use of predictive analytics for suicide is not achieved once, at deployment, and then assumed; it is maintained continuously, through governance that watches for drift, disparity, and disproportionate harm. Clinicians who understand the issues this course raises are precisely the people who should be at that governance table, ensuring that the tools serve clients rather than the reverse.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 7, title: 'Ethical and Legal Touchpoints for Algorithmic Risk Flags', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'Duty to protect', content: 'A flag is upstream of any duty; it may prompt assessment, but the clinical determination from that assessment — not the algorithm — triggers or discharges protective duties under applicable law.' },
      { title: 'Privacy and confidentiality', content: 'Clinically generated risk scores are protected health information under HIPAA; access should be limited, retention defined, and the flag prevented from becoming a permanent stigmatizing label. Externally generated scores carry different, often weaker, protections.' },
      { title: 'Consent', content: 'Monitoring of social media, searches, and devices generally lacks informed consent; transparency with clients and genuine consent, not buried terms of service, is the ethical standard.' },
      { title: 'Proportionate mandated response', content: 'Organizational protocols should match the intrusiveness of the response to the low precision of the signal — outreach and safety-plan review scale appropriately; automatic coercive responses do not.' },
      { title: 'Equity', content: 'Models can under- or over-flag specific groups by learning historical inequities; performance and calibration should be evaluated across demographic groups before and during deployment.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'text', order: 69, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Balancing Innovation Against the Precautionary Principle</h3><p>The ethics of suicide-risk prediction ultimately require holding two legitimate goods in tension. On one side is the imperative to innovate: suicide remains a leading cause of death, traditional prediction has failed, and there is a genuine moral pull toward any tool that might help identify and reach people at risk before tragedy occurs. To refuse promising tools out of excessive caution would itself be an ethical failure if those tools could save lives. On the other side is the precautionary principle: these tools are imperfect, opaque, and capable of harm — through false positives, stigma, privacy violations, inequity, and coercive responses — and deploying them carelessly could harm many people while helping few. Neither uncritical embrace nor reflexive rejection is ethically adequate; the responsible path runs between them, adopting tools that have demonstrated benefit while rigorously monitoring and minimizing their harms.</p><p>This balancing is not a one-time judgment but an ongoing ethical practice. A tool that seemed beneficial at deployment may, under monitoring, prove to impose disproportionate harm on a particular group, or to generate so many false positives that it breeds alert fatigue and dismissal, or to drift out of calibration over time. The ethical stance is to remain open to the evidence in both directions: ready to adopt and scale tools that demonstrably help, and ready to constrain or withdraw tools that prove to harm. This requires the humility to treat deployment as a hypothesis to be tested rather than a conclusion to be defended, and the institutional commitment to actually gather and act on the evidence. Clinicians, as the people closest to the human consequences of these tools, have a particular responsibility to surface harms they observe and to insist that the balancing be revisited as evidence accumulates.</p><p>For the individual clinician, the precautionary principle translates into a simple default: when uncertain about how much to trust or act on an algorithmic signal, err toward the least intrusive proportionate response and toward the human assessment that can resolve the uncertainty. The cost of a brief, careful assessment is small; the cost of an intrusive over-response to a false positive, or of a dismissive under-response to a true case, can be severe. Defaulting to careful human judgment when the algorithm's signal is uncertain — which, for a low-precision tool, is most of the time — is the practical expression of holding innovation and caution in their proper balance.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 8, question: 'How should an algorithmic suicide-risk flag relate to a clinician\'s legal duty to protect?', options: [
      { text: 'The flag itself automatically triggers the duty to protect and requires immediate protective action.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'The flag is upstream of the duty: it may prompt assessment, but a clinical determination of serious danger — not the algorithm — triggers or discharges the duty.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'The flag discharges the duty to protect because the model has already evaluated the risk.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'The flag has no relevance to the duty to protect under any circumstances.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A low-precision algorithmic flag is not a clinical judgment of serious or imminent danger. It can prompt the assessment that leads to a clinical determination, and that determination may trigger duties — but the flag neither creates nor discharges the duty by itself.' },
    { type: 'multiSelect', order: 9, question: 'Which design choices help ensure that mandated responses to risk flags remain ethical given the low precision of the signal? (Select all that apply)', options: [
      { text: 'Matching the intrusiveness of the response to the actual certainty the signal conveys', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Defaulting to involuntary evaluation for every flag to be safe', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Limiting access to flags and defining how long they are retained', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Evaluating model performance and calibration across demographic groups', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Being transparent with clients about what is monitored and why', isCorrect: true }
    ], explanation: 'Proportionate response, access limits and retention rules, equity evaluation, and transparency all align ethical practice with the low precision of the signal. Defaulting to involuntary evaluation for every flag imposes severe, disproportionate harm on the large majority of false positives.' },
    { type: 'scenarioTree', order: 10, scenarioTitle: 'A Flag Without Consent', instructions: 'Work through how a clinician responds when an externally derived risk signal arrives. Choose the most defensible next step.', startNode: 'start', nodes: {
      start: { text: 'An organizational dashboard, fed by a vendor that scores patient-portal messages, flags a client as elevated-risk. The client never consented to message scoring. What is your first move?', choices: [
        { text: 'Order an involuntary evaluation based on the flag alone.', nextId: 'over' },
        { text: 'Treat the flag as a prompt to assess, and plan to be transparent with the client about how the concern arose.', nextId: 'good' },
        { text: 'Ignore the flag because the client did not consent to scoring.', nextId: 'under' }
      ] },
      over: { text: 'Acting coercively on a low-precision, non-consented signal imposes serious harm on what is most likely a false positive and damages trust. This is the over-reaction failure mode. Reconsider.', isEnd: true },
      good: { text: 'Correct. The flag prompts a competent clinical assessment; transparency with the client about the source honors consent and the therapeutic relationship; and the clinical findings, not the algorithm, will determine any protective response.', isEnd: true },
      under: { text: 'The consent concern is real, but discarding the signal entirely forfeits genuine information and risks missing a true case. A better path uses the flag to prompt assessment while addressing the consent issue transparently. Reconsider.', isEnd: true }
    } },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 11, question: 'Consider the equity concern raised in this section. In your own setting, what would you need to know about a deployed risk model before trusting that its flags treat your clients fairly across race, language, and other dimensions — and whom would you ask?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 12, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'An algorithmic flag is upstream of the duty to protect; clinical assessment, not the model, triggers or discharges legal duties.',
      'Clinically generated risk scores are protected health information; access, retention, and the risk of permanent stigmatizing labels must be governed deliberately.',
      'Monitoring of digital life generally lacks informed consent; transparency and genuine consent are the ethical standard.',
      'Mandated responses should be proportionate to a low-precision signal, and models must be evaluated for equity across demographic groups.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ SECTION 4 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Integrating AI Flags with Clinical Judgment',
  order: 4,
  description: 'AI as prompt not verdict, structured professional judgment, collaborative safety planning',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '4', title: 'Integrating AI Flags with Clinical Judgment', subtitle: 'Structured Professional Judgment, Safety Planning, and Avoiding Both Dismissal and Over-Reaction', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>The Prompt-Not-Verdict Principle in Practice</h2><p>The previous sections built a case that an algorithmic suicide-risk flag is a low-precision statement of elevated probability, carrying ethical and legal weight only insofar as it informs clinical assessment. This section turns to the practical question that follows: how does a competent clinician actually integrate such a flag into their work? The governing principle, stated repeatedly because it is the heart of the matter, is that the flag is a prompt, not a verdict. It prompts the clinician to look, to assess, to engage — and then the clinician's structured judgment, not the algorithm, determines what happens next.</p><p>This principle has a respected ancestor in the suicide-prevention and forensic-risk literatures: structured professional judgment. Structured professional judgment occupies a deliberate middle ground between two flawed extremes. At one extreme is purely unstructured clinical judgment, which relies on the clinician's intuition alone and is vulnerable to bias, inconsistency, and the well-documented limits of unaided human prediction. At the other extreme is purely actuarial prediction, which relies on the algorithm alone and discards the rich contextual understanding that only clinical contact provides. Structured professional judgment combines them: it uses empirically informed factors to organize and discipline the clinician's evaluation, while preserving the clinician's authority to integrate context, weigh the individual case, and make the final determination. An AI flag fits naturally into this framework as one empirically derived input that informs, but does not dictate, the clinician's structured evaluation{{alert:protocol}}.</p><p>Operationally, when a flag arrives, the integrating clinician does not ask "is the algorithm right?" but rather "what does this prompt me to assess, and what do I find when I assess it?" The flag directs attention; the assessment generates the clinical picture; and the clinical picture, documented and revisited, drives care. The algorithm has done its job the moment it has prompted a competent human inquiry that would not otherwise have occurred.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Two Failure Modes: Dismissal and Over-Reaction</h3><p>Integrating AI flags well means steering between two predictable failure modes, each of which this course has named and each of which deserves vigilance. The first is dismissal. A clinician who has internalized that most flags are false may begin to treat all flags as noise, clicking past them without inquiry. This is alert fatigue expressed as clinical practice, and it is dangerous because the model, for all its imprecision, did concentrate the true cases among the flagged. Dismissal forfeits that concentration. The discipline against dismissal is simple in principle and demanding in practice: every flag earns at least a brief, documented clinical look, even when the clinician's prior is that the flag is probably false.</p><p>The second failure mode is over-reaction. A clinician anxious about liability or alarmed by the word "suicide" may treat every flag as imminent danger, escalating to coercive interventions that the actual clinical picture does not warrant. Over-reaction harms the large majority of flagged people who were never at imminent risk; it damages the therapeutic alliance; and it can deter people from honest disclosure in the future, knowing that a flag may trigger an intervention they fear. The discipline against over-reaction is to anchor the response in the assessment findings rather than the flag, and to reserve the most restrictive interventions for clinical determinations of serious, proximate danger — not for statistical signals.</p><p>The path between these failures is the same structured professional judgment described above. It treats the flag seriously enough to assess and lightly enough not to escalate on its strength alone. Clinicians who hold both disciplines simultaneously — assess every flag, escalate on findings not flags — will use these tools as they are meant to be used.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 4, calloutType: 'tip', title: 'Anchor the Response in the Assessment, Not the Flag', content: `<p>When you receive an algorithmic risk flag, let it move you to assess but never let it set your level of response. The flag tells you where to look; your assessment tells you what to do. If the assessment reveals serious, proximate danger, respond to that. If it reveals a person who is struggling but safe, respond to that. In every case, the documented clinical picture — not the algorithm's score — is what determines and justifies your action. This single habit prevents both dismissal and over-reaction.</p>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 5, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Collaborative Safety Planning as the Integration Point</h3><p>For most flags, the appropriate clinical destination is not an emergency response but a collaborative safety-planning conversation. Collaborative safety planning, as developed by Stanley and Brown{{callout:stanley-brown}} and widely adopted across suicide-prevention practice, is a brief, structured intervention in which clinician and client jointly build a written plan: recognizing personal warning signs, identifying internal coping strategies, listing people and social settings that provide distraction and support, naming individuals who can help during a crisis, identifying professional and crisis resources including the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, and reducing access to lethal means{{callout:lethal-means}}. Safety planning is collaborative by design — it is built with the person, in their words, around their life — and this collaborative character is precisely what an algorithmic flag, on its own, can never supply.</p><p>An AI flag and a safety plan complement each other well. The flag can prompt the clinician to initiate or revisit safety planning with someone who might otherwise not have been prioritized; the safety plan then does the human work the algorithm cannot. When a clinician documents that a flag prompted a safety-planning conversation, that the conversation included means-safety counseling and the 988 Lifeline, and that the plan was developed collaboratively with the client, they have integrated the algorithmic signal into genuine clinical care in a way that is both helpful to the client and defensible in the record. This is the constructive use of prediction: not to label or to coerce, but to allocate the clinician's care toward a person who can benefit from a collaborative, life-affirming plan.</p><p>It bears emphasis that safety planning is an intervention, not merely a form. Its value lies in the conversation, the specificity, and the client's authorship. A plan completed perfunctorily because a flag appeared, without genuine collaboration, captures the documentation but misses the intervention. The flag should prompt the real conversation, not a box-checking exercise.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'imageText', order: 6, title: 'A Workflow from Flag to Collaborative Plan', content: `<div class="cr-prose"><p>A sound workflow runs: a flag arrives; the clinician conducts a structured assessment; the assessment yields a clinical picture; and the clinical picture determines the response, which for most flags is a collaborative safety-planning conversation referencing 988 and means safety, and for the rare clinical determination of serious proximate danger is an appropriate higher level of care. At every step the human clinician holds authority. The algorithm contributes one input near the start and then steps aside. Documented this way, the workflow honors the prompt-not-verdict principle, avoids both failure modes, and produces a record that reflects clinical reasoning rather than algorithmic deference.</p></div>`, image: '', imageAlt: 'Flowchart from algorithmic flag through structured assessment to collaborative safety planning', imagePosition: 'right', order: 6 },
    { type: 'text', order: 67, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Clinical Encounter the Algorithm Cannot Replace</h3><p>It is worth dwelling on exactly what the clinician brings to the integration that the algorithm cannot. A risk model produces a number; a clinical encounter produces understanding. When a clinician sits with a person prompted by a flag, they assess the things that determine actual risk and that lie wholly outside any dataset: the presence and intensity of current suicidal ideation, whether there is a plan and access to means, the person's intent and ambivalence, recent precipitants such as loss or conflict, the depth of hopelessness, the strength of reasons for living, the quality of social connection, and the person's own sense of what they need. These are not data points the model weighted; they are realities the clinician discovers in relationship, through questions asked with skill and warmth and through attentive listening to what is said and unsaid. This is the irreducible human core of suicide-risk assessment, and no advance in machine learning displaces it.</p><p>The clinician also brings something the model cannot: a relationship that is itself protective. The act of reaching out, of asking directly and compassionately about suicidal thoughts, of conveying that the person matters and that help is available, is not merely assessment — it is intervention. Research and clinical experience converge on the finding that asking about suicide does not plant the idea but rather opens a door, communicating that the topic is safe to discuss and that the clinician can bear to hear it. The flag may have prompted the conversation, but the conversation itself, conducted with genuine care, does therapeutic work that no algorithm can do. The integration of an AI flag into clinical judgment is therefore not merely a matter of adding a data point to a calculation; it is a matter of using a data point to occasion a human encounter that assesses, connects, and helps.</p><p>This is why the prompt-not-verdict principle is not a grudging concession to the model's imperfection but an accurate description of the proper division of labor. The model does what it does well — scanning large populations to concentrate attention efficiently — and the clinician does what only a clinician can do: understanding a particular person in their particular circumstances and responding to them as a person rather than a score. Each contributes what the other cannot. The error to avoid is asking the model to do the clinician's work, or imagining that the clinician's work could be reduced to what the model produces. Properly integrated, the two are complementary; confused for one another, they are dangerous.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 68, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Practical Habits for Disciplined Integration</h3><p>Several concrete habits help clinicians integrate flags with judgment reliably under the pressures of real practice. The first is a standing commitment that every flag earns at least a brief, documented clinical look. Making this non-negotiable guards against alert fatigue, because it removes the moment-to-moment decision about whether a given flag is worth attention; the answer is always yes, the look is always brief, and the documentation is always made. This small structural commitment is one of the most effective protections against the dismissal failure mode, precisely because it does not rely on the clinician feeling motivated in any particular instance.</p><p>The second habit is to consciously separate the flag from the response decision. When a flag arrives, the disciplined clinician mentally sets it aside after it has prompted the assessment and asks: based on what I have actually found in this encounter, what level of response is warranted? This deliberate separation prevents the flag's emotional charge — the word "suicide," the implied urgency — from inflating the response beyond what the clinical findings support. The flag opened the inquiry; the inquiry's findings, and only those findings, set the response. Practicing this separation explicitly, even narrating it internally, builds the habit that protects against over-reaction.</p><p>The third habit is to default to the least intrusive proportionate response, escalating only as clinical findings require. For the great majority of flags, that default is a collaborative safety-planning conversation that includes the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline and addresses means safety. Reserving more restrictive interventions for clear clinical determinations of serious, proximate danger keeps the response matched to the evidence and protects the therapeutic relationship. The fourth and final habit is to document the reasoning, not the deference: a record that shows a flag prompted assessment, what the assessment found, and why the chosen response was proportionate. Clinicians who build these four habits — always look, separate flag from response, default to least-intrusive proportionate care, and document the reasoning — will integrate algorithmic flags in a manner that is clinically sound, ethically defensible, and genuinely helpful to the people they serve.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 7, title: 'Elements of a Collaborative Safety Plan', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'Warning signs', content: 'Personal thoughts, images, moods, situations, and behaviors that signal a crisis may be developing, identified in the client\'s own words.' },
      { title: 'Internal coping strategies', content: 'Things the person can do on their own to take their mind off problems without contacting another person — activities that have helped before.' },
      { title: 'Social contacts and settings for distraction', content: 'People and places that provide a sense of connection and distraction from distress, even without explicit crisis disclosure.' },
      { title: 'People to ask for help', content: 'Trusted individuals the person can reach out to for support during a developing crisis.' },
      { title: 'Professional and crisis resources', content: 'Clinicians, urgent services, and crisis lines including the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, with clear contact information.' },
      { title: 'Means safety', content: 'Collaborative steps to reduce access to lethal means, increasing the time and distance between a crisis and potential harm.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'text', order: 69, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Communicating About Flags With Clients and Teams</h3><p>An aspect of integration that is easy to overlook is communication — how a clinician talks about a flag, both with the client it concerns and with the team around them. With clients, transparency is generally the wiser course, though it must be handled with skill. If a flag prompted an outreach, a clinician can frame the contact honestly and warmly: the clinic noticed the person had not been seen in a while and wanted to check in. Where it is clinically appropriate to acknowledge that a risk-identification process surfaced their name, doing so honors the person's autonomy and avoids the corrosive dynamic of acting on secret information about them. The framing should emphasize care and connection rather than surveillance: the message is "we noticed and we wanted to make sure you are okay," not "a computer says you are dangerous." How a flag is communicated can determine whether the resulting encounter strengthens or fractures the therapeutic relationship, and clinicians should give the framing genuine thought rather than improvising in the moment.</p><p>With teams and colleagues, clarity about what a flag is and is not prevents the spread of automation bias through a clinical culture. When a flag is discussed in a team meeting or handed off across a care transition, the responsible clinician frames it accurately: this person was flagged by a population model as statistically elevated-risk over a window, an assessment was conducted, and here is what the assessment found and what the plan is. This framing keeps the team's attention on the clinical reality rather than the algorithmic label, and it models the disciplined relationship between flag and judgment that protects against both failure modes spreading through the group. A clinical culture in which flags are spoken of as verdicts will drift toward over-reaction; a culture in which they are dismissed as noise will drift toward dismissal. The language clinicians use shapes the culture, and the culture shapes how every flag is handled.</p><p>Communication also extends to the documentation handed to whoever provides care next. A clear note that distinguishes the algorithmic prompt from the clinical assessment, and that conveys the reasoning behind the chosen response, equips the next clinician to continue care thoughtfully rather than either inheriting an unexamined alarm or missing a genuine concern. Good communication about flags — with clients, with teams, and through the record — is itself a form of clinical skill in the age of predictive analytics, and it deserves the same deliberate attention as the assessment and the safety plan.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 8, question: 'Structured professional judgment, as applied to integrating an AI risk flag, is best described as:', options: [
      { text: 'Relying entirely on the algorithm\'s actuarial output because it outperforms clinicians.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Relying entirely on unstructured clinical intuition and ignoring empirical inputs.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Using empirically informed factors to organize the clinician\'s evaluation while preserving clinical authority over the final determination.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Delegating the response decision to an organizational protocol triggered by the flag.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: 'Structured professional judgment is the middle path between pure actuarial prediction and pure unstructured intuition. It uses empirical factors — including an AI flag — to discipline the evaluation while the clinician retains authority over the final, context-informed determination.' },
    { type: 'matching', order: 9, matchingInstructions: 'Match each concept to its accurate description in the integration of AI flags with clinical judgment.', matchingPairs: [
      { term: 'Prompt-not-verdict', definition: 'A flag directs the clinician to assess; the assessment, not the algorithm, decides the response' },
      { term: 'Dismissal failure mode', definition: 'Treating flags as noise and clicking past without any clinical inquiry' },
      { term: 'Over-reaction failure mode', definition: 'Escalating to coercive intervention on the strength of a flag alone' },
      { term: 'Collaborative safety planning', definition: 'A jointly authored written plan of warning signs, coping, supports, resources, and means safety' }
    ] },
    { type: 'fillInBlank', order: 10, title: 'Integration Principles', blanks: [
      { prompt: 'An AI flag should be treated as a ____, not a verdict.', answer: 'prompt', acceptAlternates: ['Prompt'] },
      { prompt: 'The middle path between pure intuition and pure actuarial prediction is structured professional ____.', answer: 'judgment', acceptAlternates: ['Judgement', 'judgement'] },
      { prompt: 'For most flags, the appropriate clinical destination is a collaborative ____ planning conversation.', answer: 'safety', acceptAlternates: ['Safety'] },
      { prompt: 'Your level of response should be anchored in the clinical ____, not in the flag.', answer: 'assessment', acceptAlternates: ['Assessment', 'findings'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'flashcardDeck', order: 70, instructions: 'Review these integration concepts that connect an AI flag to disciplined clinical action.', flashcards: [
      { id: 'f1', front: 'Prompt-not-verdict principle', back: 'A flag directs the clinician to assess; the clinical assessment, not the algorithm, determines the level and nature of the response.' },
      { id: 'f2', front: 'Structured professional judgment', back: 'The middle path between pure actuarial prediction and pure unstructured intuition; empirical inputs discipline the evaluation while the clinician retains final authority.' },
      { id: 'f3', front: 'Dismissal failure mode', back: 'Treating flags as noise and clicking past them through alert fatigue, forfeiting the real signal the model concentrates among the flagged.' },
      { id: 'f4', front: 'Over-reaction failure mode', back: 'Escalating to coercive intervention on the strength of a low-precision flag alone, harming the many false positives and the therapeutic alliance.' },
      { id: 'f5', front: 'Collaborative safety planning', back: 'A jointly authored written plan — warning signs, coping, supports, professional and crisis resources including 988, and means safety — that is the constructive destination for most flags.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 11, question: 'Recall a time you received any kind of risk alert — algorithmic or otherwise. Did you lean toward dismissal or over-reaction, and why? What structured habit could you build so that future flags reliably prompt a brief assessment without driving disproportionate response?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 12, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'An AI flag is a prompt to assess, never a verdict; structured professional judgment integrates it as one empirical input while the clinician retains final authority.',
      'The two failure modes are dismissal (forfeiting real signal through alert fatigue) and over-reaction (coercive escalation on a low-precision flag); discipline avoids both.',
      'Anchor the level of response in the documented clinical assessment, not the flag.',
      'Collaborative safety planning, referencing 988 and means safety, is the constructive integration point for most flags.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ SECTION 5 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Crisis Detection in Consumer Tech and Workflows',
  order: 5,
  description: 'Chatbot and platform crisis routing, 988 integration, organizational protocols, monitoring and QA',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '5', title: 'Crisis Detection in Consumer Tech and Workflows', subtitle: 'Platform Routing, 988 Integration, Organizational Protocols, and Quality Assurance', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>Crisis Detection Beyond the Clinic</h2><p>The settings where suicide-risk detection now operates extend far past clinical records and into the consumer technology that mediates everyday life. Search engines, social-media platforms, messaging apps, chatbots, and wearable devices all run, in various forms, systems that attempt to detect when a user may be in crisis and to route them toward help. For the clinician, these systems matter for two reasons. First, clients live within them, and a client may have already encountered a crisis-resource prompt from a platform before they ever reach a clinical encounter. Second, the design principles that make these consumer systems safe or unsafe — proportionality, accurate routing, human oversight, quality assurance — are the same principles that should govern crisis detection inside clinical organizations. Understanding the consumer landscape sharpens a clinician's judgment about workflows in their own setting.</p><p>The most visible and widely beneficial form of consumer crisis detection is resource surfacing. When a person searches for terms associated with suicide or posts content expressing suicidal thoughts, many platforms now respond by prominently displaying crisis resources — in the United States, the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, reachable by call or text at 988 and by chat through its website. This is a low-risk, high-value intervention: it imposes minimal cost on false positives (a person who was not in crisis simply sees a resource banner) while reliably connecting people in distress to help at the exact moment they reach out. The 988 transition, which replaced the older ten-digit Lifeline number with a three-digit code, was designed precisely to make this connection easier, and platform integration of 988 has extended its reach into the digital spaces where distress is often first expressed.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Chatbots, Conversational AI, and the Routing Imperative</h3><p>The rapid spread of conversational AI — chatbots and large-language-model assistants used for general purposes and, increasingly, for emotional support — has created a new and consequential frontier for crisis detection. People disclose distress to chatbots, sometimes more readily than to humans, precisely because the chatbot feels non-judgmental and always available. This makes the chatbot a place where suicidal ideation may surface, and it places a heavy responsibility on the chatbot's designers to detect such disclosures and route the person to appropriate human help rather than attempting to manage a crisis themselves. A conversational system is not a crisis counselor, and the safe design pattern is detection plus routing: recognize potential crisis language, surface the 988 Lifeline and other crisis resources clearly, and, where appropriate and consented, facilitate connection to human support. A chatbot that attempts to substitute for crisis care, or that fails to recognize and route a clear disclosure, can cause serious harm.</p><p>The routing imperative also illuminates a key limitation of all automated crisis detection in conversational settings: language is ambiguous, context-dependent, and easily misread by systems that lack genuine understanding. A person quoting song lyrics, discussing a news story, or using dark humor may trip a detector; a person in genuine crisis may express it obliquely and be missed. This is the familiar tension between sensitivity and specificity, now playing out in real-time conversation. The responsible response is not to demand perfect detection — which is impossible — but to pair detection with low-harm, easily dismissible resource surfacing for the many ambiguous cases, and to reserve any higher-intensity routing for clearer signals, ideally with human review before consequential action.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 4, calloutType: 'protocol', title: 'The Safe Pattern: Detect, Surface 988, Route to Humans', content: `<p>Across consumer technology and clinical workflows alike, the safe crisis-detection pattern is consistent: detect possible crisis signals, surface the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline and other resources clearly and accessibly, and route the person toward trained human help rather than letting an automated system manage the crisis. Automated detection allocates attention and connects people to resources; trained humans — crisis counselors, clinicians — do the actual crisis work. Any workflow that lets an algorithm substitute for human crisis response, or that escalates to coercive action without human review, departs from this safe pattern.</p>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 5, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Organizational Protocols and 988 Integration in Clinical Settings</h3><p>Inside clinical organizations, the lessons of consumer crisis detection translate into protocol design. When a health system or behavioral-health organization deploys any crisis-detection capability — an EHR risk flag, a portal-message scanner, a crisis-line triage tool — it must wrap that capability in a clear protocol that specifies who is notified, what they are expected to do, how quickly, and with what documentation. A detection system without a defined human response protocol is worse than no system, because it generates signals that no one is accountable for acting on. The protocol should embody the principles built throughout this course: proportionate response matched to a low-precision signal, structured assessment rather than automatic escalation, collaborative safety planning as the default destination, and explicit incorporation of the 988 Lifeline as a resource for clients between or after contacts.</p><p>Integration of 988 into clinical workflows is itself an important design choice. The Lifeline provides twenty-four-hour crisis support and, in many areas, coordination with local crisis services, and it can serve as a bridge for clients who experience distress outside of business hours or between appointments. Building 988 into safety plans, into after-hours messaging, into discharge instructions, and into the resources surfaced by any organizational detection system extends the reach of care beyond the clinician's direct availability. The clinician who reflexively includes 988 in every safety plan and crisis-related communication is practicing in line with current standards and giving clients a reliable point of contact when the clinician cannot be reached.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 6, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Monitoring and Quality Assurance</h3><p>No crisis-detection system should be deployed and then forgotten. The performance characteristics established earlier — sensitivity, specificity, calibration — are not fixed properties; they drift as populations, language, documentation, and the underlying clinical environment change. Responsible deployment therefore includes ongoing monitoring and quality assurance: tracking how many flags are generated, how many are acted upon, what proportion correspond to genuine clinical concern on assessment, how the system performs across demographic groups, and whether alert fatigue is degrading clinician response. Quality assurance also attends to the human side of the workflow: are clinicians completing the assessments that flags prompt? Are safety plans being built collaboratively or perfunctorily? Are responses proportionate, or has the organization drifted toward either dismissal or over-reaction? A detection system is only as good as the human workflow surrounding it, and that workflow requires the same continuous-improvement attention that any clinical process does.</p><p>Quality assurance has an ethical dimension as well. Monitoring whether a system over-flags or under-flags specific groups is how equity concerns are caught and corrected in operation rather than assumed away at deployment. Monitoring the downstream consequences of flags — how often a flag led to a beneficial outreach versus an intrusive, unnecessary intervention — is how an organization holds itself accountable for the real-world impact of its detection systems on the people they are meant to serve. The clinician's role in this is partly individual, in conscientious response to each flag, and partly collective, in participating in and advocating for the organizational monitoring that keeps these systems safe and fair over time.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 67, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The 988 Transition and What It Changed</h3><p>The 2022 transition to 988 as the national three-digit code for the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline was more than a change of phone number; it reflected a broader reorientation of crisis care that clinicians should understand because it shapes the workflows they build and the resources they offer. The older Lifeline operated through a ten-digit number that many people did not know and could not recall in a moment of distress. Replacing it with an easy-to-remember three-digit code lowered the barrier to reaching help, and the transition was accompanied by federal investment in the crisis-care system more broadly: in the network of local crisis centers that answer 988 contacts, in mobile crisis teams that can respond in person, and in crisis-stabilization settings that offer an alternative to emergency departments and law enforcement for people in psychiatric crisis. The vision behind 988 is a crisis-care continuum in which someone to call, someone to respond, and somewhere to go are all available without defaulting to police or emergency rooms.</p><p>For clinicians integrating detection systems, the 988 transition has practical implications. It means that 988 is now a uniformly available, easy-to-communicate resource that belongs in every safety plan, every after-hours message, and every resource a detection system surfaces. It means that a person reached by an organizational outreach can be given a single, memorable point of contact for the moments between clinical encounters. And it means that the resources surfaced by any consumer or clinical crisis-detection system should route toward 988 and the crisis continuum it anchors, rather than defaulting reflexively to emergency services, which are appropriate for some situations but are often a disproportionate and even harmful response to many crises. The clinician who understands the 988 system can help clients access a response that is matched to their need, neither too little nor too coercive.</p><p>It bears emphasis that 988 is staffed by trained crisis counselors, which connects directly to the safe-pattern principle established earlier: detection allocates attention and connects people to resources, but trained humans do the crisis work. When a detection system surfaces 988, it is routing a person not to another algorithm but to a trained human counselor, which is exactly the handoff the safe pattern prescribes. This is why building 988 into workflows is not merely a compliance gesture but a substantive embodiment of the principle that automated detection should connect people to human help rather than attempt to manage crises itself.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 68, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Special Problem of Welfare Checks and Coercive Routing</h3><p>One of the most ethically fraught features of some consumer crisis-detection systems is their capacity to escalate beyond resource surfacing to actively dispatching emergency services for a welfare check. When a platform infers, from a post or message, that a person may be in imminent danger and routes that inference to law enforcement or emergency medical services, the person is subjected to an in-person intervention they did not request, based on a low-precision inference, often without their knowledge or consent. The potential harms are serious: an unexpected and frightening encounter, loss of privacy, the trauma of a coercive response, and in the worst cases, escalation of a police encounter into danger, particularly for members of communities who have reason to fear such encounters. These harms fall, by the arithmetic of false positives, overwhelmingly on people who were not in imminent danger.</p><p>This is not an argument that no situation ever warrants an emergency response; some do, and detection systems can occasionally connect a person to life-saving help they could not reach themselves. It is an argument that coercive routing must be reserved for the clearest signals, must wherever possible involve human review before consequential action, and must be designed with full awareness of the harms it imposes on the many false positives it will inevitably generate. A system that dispatches welfare checks liberally on the strength of automated inference is applying the over-reaction failure mode at population scale, imposing intrusive and sometimes dangerous interventions on a large majority of people who did not need them. The same proportionality principle that governs an individual clinician's response to a flag governs the design of these consumer systems: the intrusiveness of the response must match the certainty and severity the signal actually conveys, and an automated inference from a post conveys little of either.</p><p>Clinicians who become aware that a client experienced such an intervention should approach it with sensitivity, recognizing that the experience may have been distressing or alienating and may color the client's willingness to disclose distress in digital spaces in the future. More broadly, clinicians who participate in the governance of organizational detection systems should advocate for the least coercive effective response, for human review before consequential action, and for honest accounting of the harms that liberal coercive routing imposes. The goal of crisis detection is to help people in distress reach care, and a system that frightens or traumatizes the people it flags — most of whom were not in danger — has lost sight of that goal.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'videoEmbed', order: 7, videoTitle: 'Crisis Routing, 988, and Safe Workflow Design', videoUrl: 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/PLACEHOLDER_suiciderisk', description: 'How consumer platforms and clinical organizations design crisis-detection workflows that surface the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, route to trained humans, and apply ongoing quality assurance.' },
    { type: 'text', order: 69, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Human Workflow Is the System</h3><p>A theme that runs through everything in this section deserves explicit statement: a crisis-detection system is not the algorithm; it is the entire socio-technical workflow of which the algorithm is one part. The detector generates a signal, but the signal accomplishes nothing on its own. Whether a flagged person receives helpful, proportionate care depends on who is notified, how quickly they respond, whether they conduct a competent assessment, whether they default to collaborative safety planning with 988, whether they document their reasoning, and whether the organization monitors all of this and corrects course when it drifts. The algorithm is, in a sense, the least important part of the system; the human workflow surrounding it determines whether it helps or harms. An excellent model wired into a broken workflow — one with no clear response, no accountability, no proportionality, and no monitoring — will produce worse outcomes than no model at all, because it will generate signals that are either ignored or mishandled while creating the illusion that something systematic is being done.</p><p>This reframing has practical consequences for how clinicians and organizations should evaluate any crisis-detection capability they are considering or already using. The question is never simply "is the model accurate?" but "is the whole workflow sound?" Does a flag reliably reach a person who will act on it appropriately? Is that person trained and resourced to conduct the assessment the flag prompts? Is the default response proportionate and humane? Is 988 woven throughout? Is the system monitored for performance, equity, and alert fatigue, with someone accountable for acting on what the monitoring reveals? A capability that cannot answer these questions affirmatively is not ready for deployment regardless of how impressive its model metrics appear. The work of building a safe crisis-detection system is overwhelmingly the work of building the human workflow, and that work is where clinicians' expertise and advocacy are most needed.</p><p>For the individual clinician embedded in such a system, the implication is empowering: you are not a passive recipient of algorithmic outputs but an essential component of the system's safety. The quality of your assessment when a flag arrives, your fidelity to proportionate response, your consistent integration of 988 into safety planning, and your willingness to surface problems you observe in the workflow all directly determine whether the system serves the people it is meant to protect. The algorithm cannot do your part, and your part is the part that turns a signal into care. Recognizing this — that the human workflow is the system, and that you are an indispensable part of it — is the practical foundation for using these tools well.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 8, title: 'Quality-Assurance Questions for Any Crisis-Detection Workflow', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'Is there a defined human response protocol?', content: 'Every detection capability must specify who is notified, what they do, how fast, and with what documentation. A signal no one is accountable for is worse than no signal.' },
      { title: 'Is the response proportionate to the signal?', content: 'Low-precision flags should default to outreach and collaborative safety planning, with coercive responses reserved for clinical determinations of serious, proximate danger.' },
      { title: 'Is 988 integrated throughout?', content: 'The 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline should appear in safety plans, after-hours communications, and any resource the system surfaces, giving clients a reliable contact point.' },
      { title: 'Is performance monitored over time and across groups?', content: 'Sensitivity, specificity, calibration, and equity drift; ongoing monitoring catches degradation and disparate impact rather than assuming them away.' },
      { title: 'Is alert fatigue being tracked?', content: 'If clinicians are clicking past flags without assessment, the workflow is failing regardless of model quality; QA must surface and address this.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 9, question: 'What is the recommended "safe pattern" for crisis detection in both consumer technology and clinical workflows?', options: [
      { text: 'Let the automated system manage the crisis conversation to completion without human involvement.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Detect possible crisis signals, surface 988 and other resources clearly, and route the person to trained human help.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Escalate every detection directly to emergency services without review.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Suppress crisis resources to avoid alarming users who may be false positives.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'The safe pattern is detect, surface 988 and resources, and route to trained humans. Automated systems allocate attention and connect people to help; trained humans do the crisis work. Neither letting an algorithm manage a crisis nor escalating to emergency services without review is safe.' },
    { type: 'cardSort', order: 10, instructions: 'Sort each practice into whether it strengthens or undermines a safe crisis-detection workflow.', categories: ['Strengthens', 'Undermines'], cards: [
      { id: 'c1', text: 'Surfacing 988 clearly when crisis language is detected', correctCategory: 'Strengthens' },
      { id: 'c2', text: 'Letting a chatbot attempt to manage an active suicidal crisis alone', correctCategory: 'Undermines' },
      { id: 'c3', text: 'Defining a human response protocol with accountability and documentation', correctCategory: 'Strengthens' },
      { id: 'c4', text: 'Deploying a detector and never monitoring its performance again', correctCategory: 'Undermines' },
      { id: 'c5', text: 'Tracking alert fatigue and equity across demographic groups', correctCategory: 'Strengthens' },
      { id: 'c6', text: 'Escalating every flag to coercive intervention without assessment', correctCategory: 'Undermines' }
    ] },
    { type: 'multiSelect', order: 11, question: 'Which elements belong in a responsible organizational crisis-detection protocol? (Select all that apply)', options: [
      { text: 'A defined human response: who is notified, what they do, and how quickly', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Proportionate response matched to the low precision of the signal', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Explicit integration of the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline', isCorrect: true },
      { text: 'Automatic involuntary action triggered by any flag', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Ongoing monitoring of performance, equity, and alert fatigue', isCorrect: true }
    ], explanation: 'A defined human response, proportionality, 988 integration, and ongoing monitoring are all essential. Automatic involuntary action on any flag is the over-reaction failure mode applied at the organizational level and imposes disproportionate harm.' },
    { type: 'reflection', order: 12, question: 'Evaluate a crisis-detection workflow you have encountered — clinical or consumer-facing — against the quality-assurance questions in this section. Where is it strong, and where would you push for improvement, especially around 988 integration and proportionate human response?' },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 13, title: 'Key Takeaways', takeaways: [
      'Consumer platforms detect crisis signals and surface resources such as 988; the safe pattern everywhere is detect, surface 988, and route to trained humans.',
      'Conversational AI must route disclosures to human help, not attempt to manage a crisis itself, given the ambiguity of language and the limits of detection.',
      'Clinical organizations must wrap any detector in a defined, proportionate human response protocol with 988 integrated throughout.',
      'Ongoing monitoring and quality assurance — including equity and alert-fatigue tracking — are essential because performance and human response both drift over time.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ SECTION 6 ============================
COURSE.sections.push({
  title: 'Applied Case, Resources, and Commitments',
  order: 6,
  description: 'Case application, suicide-prevention resources, and commitments to practice',
  contentBlocks: [
    { type: 'sectionDivider', sectionNumber: '6', title: 'Applied Case, Resources, and Commitments', subtitle: 'Bringing Prediction, Limits, Ethics, and Integration Together in Practice', order: 1 },
    { type: 'text', order: 2, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h2>An Integrated Case</h2><p>Consider Marcus, a forty-one-year-old veteran enrolled in care at a large health system. On a Monday morning, a coordinator receives a notification: a population-level risk model{{callout:population-risk-model}}, run over the weekend, has placed Marcus in the highest-risk group it flags each month. Marcus has not been seen in the clinic in four months. The flag is a statement of elevated probability over a window — not a verdict, not an emergency, and most likely, on its own, a false positive. But it is also genuine information: the model has concentrated true risk among the people it flags, and Marcus's months-long absence from care is exactly the kind of disengagement the program was built to address.</p><p>The coordinator does not order an involuntary evaluation; that would be the over-reaction failure mode, imposing serious harm on what is probably a false positive. Nor does the coordinator dismiss the flag as statistical noise; that would be the dismissal failure mode, forfeiting the real information the model carries. Instead, the coordinator does what the prompt-not-verdict principle prescribes: treats the flag as a reason to reach out and assess. A phone call is placed. Marcus answers, and the coordinator, warm and direct, explains that the clinic noticed he had not been in for a while and wanted to check in and reconnect him with care.</p></div>`, callouts: { 'population-risk-model': { label: 'Population-Level Risk Model', type: 'definition', body: 'A statistical or machine-learning model that scores an entire enrolled population on a recurring schedule and surfaces a small high-risk group for proactive human follow-up, rather than waiting for a person in crisis to present.' } } },
    { type: 'text', order: 3, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>From Flag to Collaborative Plan</h3><p>In the conversation, Marcus discloses that the past two months have been hard — sleep is poor, he has withdrawn from friends, and he has had passing thoughts that his family would be better off without him, though he has no plan and does not want to die. This is precisely the kind of clinical picture that only a human encounter can establish; the algorithm could never have told the coordinator any of it. The structured assessment that the flag prompted now generates the clinical reality that will determine the response. The coordinator, finding distress and passive ideation but no imminent danger, does not escalate to emergency services. The coordinator does what the assessment warrants: schedules a prompt clinical appointment and, in the meantime, conducts a collaborative safety-planning conversation.</p><p>Together, Marcus and the clinician build a plan in Marcus's own words: his warning signs (poor sleep, isolating, the better-off-without-me thought), internal coping strategies that have helped before, friends and settings that provide connection, people he can call, professional contacts, and — critically — the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, which Marcus can reach by call or text at any hour. They discuss means safety collaboratively. The clinician documents that the algorithmic flag prompted outreach, that a structured assessment found passive ideation without imminent danger, that a collaborative safety plan including 988 was developed, and that a follow-up appointment was scheduled. The flag did its job: it allocated clinical attention to a disengaged veteran who benefited from a life-affirming, collaborative plan. The human did the rest, as it must.</p><p>This case embodies every principle of the course. The flag was a prompt, not a verdict. The clinician avoided both dismissal and over-reaction. The response was proportionate to a low-precision signal. The 988 Lifeline was integrated into a collaborative safety plan. And the documentation reflected clinical reasoning rather than algorithmic deference. This is what competent integration of AI risk prediction looks like in practice.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 65, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>A Contrasting Case: When the Same Flag Is Mishandled</h3><p>To sharpen the lessons of the Marcus case, consider how the same flag could have produced harm in the hands of a clinician who had not internalized this course's principles. Imagine that, instead of the measured coordinator, Marcus's flag had reached a clinician anxious about liability and alarmed by the word "suicide." This clinician, treating the flag as a verdict of imminent danger, contacts Marcus and, before any real assessment, arranges for an emergency evaluation, perhaps even involving law enforcement to ensure he is brought in. Marcus, who has passive ideation but no plan and no intent to die, experiences this as a terrifying overreaction to a check-in he did not request. He feels surveilled and pathologized. The encounter that could have rebuilt his connection to care instead ruptures it, and Marcus resolves never again to be honest about his thoughts with anyone in the health system. This is the over-reaction failure mode, and its cost is borne entirely by a person who was, by the model's own arithmetic, most likely a false positive.</p><p>Now imagine a different mishandling. A clinician who has heard that risk flags are usually false, and who is overwhelmed and behind, sees Marcus's flag and clicks past it. No outreach is made. Marcus, disengaged from care for months and quietly worsening, receives no contact. The flag, which had genuinely concentrated risk and identified a person who would benefit from re-engagement, is wasted. This is the dismissal failure mode, and its cost is the forfeiture of an opportunity the system existed to create. Both mishandlings flow from the same root error: treating the flag as something it is not — a verdict in the first case, noise in the second — rather than as what it is, a prompt to conduct a competent, proportionate clinical assessment.</p><p>The contrast illuminates why the disciplined middle path is not merely a tidy compromise but the only response that honors both the person and the information. The flag carries real but limited information; it deserves to be neither inflated into a crisis nor discarded as nothing. The clinician who reaches out, assesses, finds what is actually there, and responds proportionately — defaulting to collaborative safety planning with 988 — extracts the genuine value of the flag while imposing minimal harm. Every principle in this course converges on that single disciplined act.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'text', order: 66, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>The Counselor's Enduring Role</h3><p>It would be easy, surveying the rise of predictive analytics and crisis detection, to feel that the clinician's role is being encroached upon — that algorithms are taking over a domain that was once purely human. This course argues for the opposite conclusion. The proliferation of these tools makes the skilled clinician more essential, not less, because every output these systems produce requires human interpretation, contextualization, and judgment to become anything useful or safe. A flag with no clinician to assess it is noise. A detection system with no human response protocol is worse than nothing. A risk score with no one to integrate it into a collaborative, relational plan of care is an empty number. The tools generate signals; only clinicians turn signals into care.</p><p>The skills this course has emphasized — statistical literacy to interpret performance honestly, ethical reasoning to navigate duty and privacy and consent and equity, and the clinical wisdom of structured professional judgment and collaborative safety planning — are precisely the skills that the age of algorithmic risk prediction demands. They are not made obsolete by the tools; they are the indispensable complement to them. A clinician who possesses these skills can take the genuine value that predictive analytics offer — the efficient concentration of attention toward people who might otherwise go unnoticed — and convert it into the human work of assessment, connection, and life-affirming planning that no algorithm can perform. That conversion is the counselor's enduring role, and it is more important, not less, in a world where risk signals arrive from records, phones, platforms, and crisis lines.</p><p>The person on the other side of every flag is not a score. They are someone in distress, or someone who might be, who deserves to be met by a human being who will assess with care, respond with proportion, and offer a plan for staying safe and connected that includes a reliable resource — the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline — for the hardest moments. The tools can help find that person. Only the clinician can meet them. Holding that distinction clearly, and practicing accordingly, is the whole of what this course has asked.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'callout', order: 4, calloutType: 'key', title: 'The Whole Course in One Sentence', content: `<p>An algorithmic suicide-risk flag is a low-precision, low-base-rate signal that should prompt — never replace — a competent, ethically grounded clinical assessment, integrated through structured professional judgment into collaborative safety planning that references the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, with proportionate response, equity, and ongoing quality assurance throughout.</p>` },
    { type: 'imageText', order: 5, title: 'Carrying the Framework Into Your Setting', content: `<div class="cr-prose"><p>Whether or not your organization currently deploys a predictive model, the framework of this course applies wherever risk signals enter your practice. Know where the signal comes from and what it can and cannot do. Interpret its performance honestly, remembering that even excellent models produce mostly false positives for a rare outcome. Hold the ethical line on duty, privacy, consent, and equity. Let every flag prompt assessment, escalate only on clinical findings, and make collaborative safety planning with 988 your default destination. These habits are durable regardless of which specific tool a vendor or system places in front of you.</p></div>`, image: '', imageAlt: 'A clinician integrating an algorithmic risk flag into collaborative, human-led care', imagePosition: 'left', order: 5 },
    { type: 'text', order: 64, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Bringing the Six Sections Together</h3><p>This final section asks you to hold the whole course at once, because competent practice with these tools requires integrating every theme rather than applying any one in isolation. Recall the arc. Section 1 established what predictive analytics for suicide actually are: machine-learning and statistical models, drawing on electronic records, digital signals, and crisis-line text, that combine many weak signals to estimate elevated risk over a window, exemplified by operational systems such as REACH VET and the Mental Health Research Network models. Section 2 confronted the hard mathematics of these tools: even excellent models, because suicide is a low-base-rate outcome, produce mostly false positives, a reality documented by Belsher and colleagues and unavoidable regardless of how good the algorithm is. Section 3 mapped the ethical and legal terrain: a flag is upstream of any duty to protect, risk scores are sensitive and stigmatizing, monitoring often lacks consent, mandated responses must be proportionate, and models must be evaluated for equity.</p><p>Section 4 turned to integration, establishing the prompt-not-verdict principle and the discipline of structured professional judgment that steers between the failure modes of dismissal and over-reaction, with collaborative safety planning as the constructive destination for most flags. Section 5 extended these principles to crisis detection in consumer technology and clinical workflows, establishing the safe pattern of detect, surface 988, and route to trained humans, and insisting that the human workflow surrounding any detector is the real system. And this final section has shown, through the contrasting fortunes of the Marcus case, how every principle converges on a single disciplined act: use the flag to prompt a competent, proportionate, human assessment, and let that assessment, integrated into collaborative safety planning with 988, determine the care.</p><p>No single section suffices alone. Statistical literacy without ethical reasoning produces a clinician who interprets the numbers correctly but mishandles privacy or equity. Ethical reasoning without statistical literacy produces a clinician who cannot tell whether a flag deserves trust. Either without the clinical wisdom of structured professional judgment and collaborative safety planning produces a clinician who understands the tool but cannot translate it into care. The competence this course builds is the integration of all three — the honest interpretation of imperfect signals, the principled navigation of their ethical stakes, and the relational clinical skill that turns a signal into help for a person in distress. That integrated competence is what the people on the other side of every flag deserve.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'accordion', order: 6, title: 'A Practitioner\'s Checklist for AI Risk Flags', accordionItems: [
      { title: 'Understand the source', content: 'Know what data the flag is built from, what window it predicts, and whether it is clinically or externally generated.' },
      { title: 'Interpret performance honestly', content: 'Expect low positive predictive value for a rare outcome; ask about calibration and equity, not just AUC.' },
      { title: 'Honor ethics and law', content: 'Treat the flag as upstream of any duty, protect the privacy of scores, attend to consent, and demand equity evaluation.' },
      { title: 'Assess, do not defer', content: 'Let the flag prompt a structured assessment; let the assessment, not the algorithm, set the response.' },
      { title: 'Plan collaboratively with 988', content: 'Default to collaborative safety planning that includes the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline and means safety.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'text', order: 67, content: `<div class="cr-prose"><h3>Looking Ahead: Stewardship of an Evolving Technology</h3><p>Predictive analytics for suicide and crisis detection are not static technologies; they are evolving rapidly, and the clinician's relationship to them must be one of ongoing stewardship rather than one-time acceptance or rejection. New models will be developed, validated, and deployed. Conversational AI will become more capable and more deeply embedded in the spaces where people express distress. Digital phenotyping will draw on richer streams of behavioral data. Each advance will bring genuine potential benefits and genuine new risks, and the framework this course has built is designed to remain useful across that evolution precisely because it is grounded not in the details of any particular tool but in durable principles: understand the source and the data, interpret performance honestly, hold the ethical line on duty and privacy and consent and equity, treat flags as prompts and not verdicts, and integrate them through human judgment into collaborative, proportionate care.</p><p>Stewardship implies responsibility for outcomes, not just for procedures. As these tools spread, clinicians have a role in ensuring that they actually reduce suffering and save lives rather than merely generating activity, alarm, and the appearance of action. That role includes asking whether a deployed tool has demonstrated benefit in a population like the one it serves, whether its harms are being monitored and minimized, and whether its burdens and benefits are distributed equitably. It includes resisting the temptation to adopt a tool simply because it is available or because it offers the comfortable feeling of having done something systematic about a frightening problem. And it includes advocating, in the governance settings where clinicians have voice, for the proportionate, monitored, equitable workflows that distinguish responsible use from reckless deployment.</p><p>The deepest commitment this course asks is to keep the person at the center as the technology evolves. Every model, every flag, every detection system exists, or should exist, to serve a human being in distress. The measure of these tools is not their statistical elegance or their scale but whether they help clinicians reach, understand, and care for people who might otherwise have suffered alone. A clinician who holds that measure steadily — who lets it govern which tools to trust, how to interpret their outputs, and how to respond — will be a good steward of predictive analytics in suicide prevention, whatever specific forms the technology takes in the years ahead. That stewardship, exercised one flag and one encounter at a time, is how the promise of these tools is realized and their dangers held in check.</p></div>` },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', order: 7, question: 'In the case of Marcus, which action best reflects the prompt-not-verdict principle taught in this course?', options: [
      { text: 'Ordering an involuntary evaluation immediately upon seeing the flag.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Dismissing the flag as statistical noise and taking no action.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Using the flag as a reason to reach out, conducting a structured assessment, and letting the clinical findings determine a proportionate response.', isCorrect: false },
      { text: 'Forwarding the flag to emergency services without contacting Marcus.', isCorrect: false }
    ], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: 'The prompt-not-verdict principle uses the flag to trigger outreach and structured assessment, then anchors the response in the clinical findings. The coordinator avoided both over-reaction (involuntary evaluation) and dismissal (no action), and responded proportionately to what the assessment revealed.' },
    { type: 'sequencing', order: 8, instructions: 'Order the steps of the integrated workflow demonstrated in the Marcus case, from first to last.', steps: [
      { id: 's1', text: 'A population-level model flags the client as elevated-risk over a defined window.', order: 1 },
      { id: 's2', text: 'A coordinator treats the flag as a prompt and conducts proactive outreach.', order: 2 },
      { id: 's3', text: 'A structured clinical assessment establishes the actual clinical picture.', order: 3 },
      { id: 's4', text: 'A collaborative safety plan including 988 is built, and proportionate follow-up is documented.', order: 4 }
    ], explanation: 'The flag prompts outreach; outreach enables assessment; assessment determines a proportionate response; and collaborative safety planning with 988, with clear documentation, completes the integration. At every step the human clinician holds authority.' },
    { type: 'multipleChoice', question: "In the Marcus case used to integrate this course’s lessons, what made the clinician’s response effective?", options: [{ text: "Relying on the algorithmic flag alone to determine the treatment plan", isCorrect: false }, { text: "Treating the flag as a hypothesis to explore collaboratively with Marcus, integrating it with clinical judgment", isCorrect: true }, { text: "Dismissing the flag because algorithms are known to be unreliable", isCorrect: false }, { text: "Informing Marcus that the system had already decided he was high-risk", isCorrect: false }], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: "The course’s central lesson is that a predictive flag is a hypothesis, not a verdict; the effective response integrates the flag with direct clinical engagement and judgment rather than either deferring to it blindly or ignoring it." },
{ type: 'reflection', order: 9, question: 'Name one realistic situation in your own practice where an algorithmic or system-generated risk flag could appear. Walk through, in writing, how you would apply the prompt-not-verdict principle, avoid both failure modes, and integrate 988 into collaborative safety planning.' },
    { type: 'resources', order: 10, title: 'Suicide Prevention & Crisis Resources', resources: [
      { title: '988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline', url: 'https://988lifeline.org', type: 'link', description: 'Free, confidential, 24/7 crisis support across the United States by call or text to 988 and by online chat. The central resource to integrate into every safety plan.' },
      { title: 'SAMHSA — Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration', url: 'https://www.samhsa.gov', type: 'link', description: 'Federal agency leading public-health efforts on mental health and substance use, including 988 implementation, crisis-system guidance, and the SAMHSA National Helpline.' },
      { title: 'CDC — Suicide Prevention', url: 'https://www.cdc.gov/suicide', type: 'link', description: 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention resources on suicide epidemiology, risk and protective factors, and the public-health approach to prevention.' },
      { title: 'AFSP — American Foundation for Suicide Prevention', url: 'https://afsp.org', type: 'link', description: 'National organization funding research, education, and advocacy in suicide prevention, with clinician and public resources.' },
      { title: 'Zero Suicide in Health and Behavioral Health Care', url: 'https://zerosuicide.edc.org', type: 'link', description: 'A framework and toolkit for systematic, system-wide suicide prevention in health and behavioral-health organizations, including screening, risk formulation, safety planning, and care transitions.' },
      { title: 'NIMH — Suicide Prevention', url: 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/suicide-prevention', type: 'link', description: 'National Institute of Mental Health information on suicide prevention research, warning signs, and evidence-based resources for professionals and the public.' },
      { title: 'Stanley-Brown Safety Planning Intervention', url: 'https://suicidesafetyplan.com', type: 'link', description: 'The evidence-based collaborative safety-planning intervention referenced throughout this course, with training and materials for clinicians.' },
      { title: 'Veterans Crisis Line', url: 'https://www.veteranscrisisline.net', type: 'link', description: 'Confidential crisis support for veterans, service members, and their families; reachable by dialing 988 then pressing 1, by text, and by chat.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'keyTakeaway', order: 11, title: 'Three Commitments to Practice', takeaways: [
      'I will treat every algorithmic or system-generated suicide-risk flag as a prompt to conduct or update a structured clinical assessment — never as a verdict — and I will anchor my level of response in the documented clinical findings rather than the flag.',
      'I will interpret risk-model performance honestly, remembering that even strong models produce mostly false positives for a rare outcome, and I will ask about calibration and equity before relying on any tool in my setting.',
      'I will make collaborative safety planning that includes the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline my default destination for flagged clients, avoiding both dismissal and over-reaction, and I will advocate for proportionate, monitored, equitable workflows where I practice.'
    ] }
  ]
});

// ============================ ASSESSMENT ============================
COURSE.assessment.questions = [
  { question: 'The Franklin et al. (2017) meta-analysis of fifty years of research found that the predictive accuracy of individual suicide risk factors was:', options: [
    { text: 'Highly accurate and steadily improving across the decades reviewed', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Only marginally better than chance and not meaningfully improved over fifty years', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Perfect for predicting the exact timing of suicide attempts', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Irrelevant because single risk factors were never studied', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Franklin and colleagues found individual risk factors predicted suicidal thoughts and behaviors only marginally better than chance, with no meaningful improvement across fifty years — the motivation behind combining many weak signals via machine learning.' },
  { question: 'REACH VET, the VA suicide-prevention program, is best described as:', options: [
    { text: 'A monthly population scan that flags the highest-risk veterans for proactive clinician outreach and care re-engagement', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'An automated system that initiates involuntary holds for high-scoring veterans', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'A self-referral crisis line for veterans only', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'A replacement for clinical suicide-risk assessment', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: 'REACH VET scores the enrolled veteran population monthly, flags the highest-risk fraction, and assigns a coordinator for outreach and re-engagement. It is a care-coordination prompt, not an automatic intervention or a replacement for assessment.' },
  { question: 'Which research line pioneered the application of natural language processing to social-media text for suicide-risk signals?', options: [
    { text: 'Belsher and colleagues', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Coppersmith and colleagues', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Stanley and Brown', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Kirkpatrick and colleagues', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Coppersmith and colleagues pioneered NLP analysis of social-media language for markers of suicidal ideation and risk, giving rise to broader digital-phenotyping approaches.' },
  { question: 'Sensitivity, as a performance metric, refers to:', options: [
    { text: 'The proportion of non-cases the model correctly clears', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'The proportion of true cases the model correctly flags', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'The probability that a flagged person actually has the outcome', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Whether predicted probabilities match observed frequencies', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Sensitivity (recall) is the proportion of people who will have the outcome whom the model correctly flags. Specificity concerns non-cases, PPV concerns the meaning of a flag, and calibration concerns probability accuracy.' },
  { question: 'In a population of 100,000 with a suicide-attempt base rate of 1 in 1,000, a model with 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity yields a positive predictive value that is:', options: [
    { text: 'Above 90%, because sensitivity is high', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Under 2%, because ~4,995 false positives swamp the ~90 true cases caught', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Exactly 50%, by definition', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Impossible to estimate without the AUC', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'The 5% false-positive rate applied to ~99,900 non-cases produces ~4,995 false alarms, versus ~90 true cases caught, giving a PPV under 2%. This is the low-base-rate problem.' },
  { question: 'The Belsher et al. (2019) JAMA Psychiatry systematic review concluded that suicide-prediction models:', options: [
    { text: 'Should immediately replace clinical assessment at the point of care', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Had uniformly low positive predictive values and should not be used as standalone clinical decision tools', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Predicted suicide with near-perfect precision', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Were no better than chance at discrimination', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Belsher and colleagues found reasonable discrimination but uniformly low PPVs across models and cautioned against using their outputs as standalone clinical decision tools at the point of care.' },
  { question: 'Calibration, as distinct from discrimination, asks:', options: [
    { text: 'Whether the model can separate higher-risk from lower-risk people', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Whether the model\'s predicted probabilities match observed frequencies', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Whether the model has a high AUC', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Whether the model was trained on enough data', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Calibration concerns whether, when a model predicts a 5% probability, about 5% of such people actually have the outcome. A model can discriminate well yet be miscalibrated, and calibration can drift over time.' },
  { question: 'Alert fatigue in the context of suicide-risk flags refers to:', options: [
    { text: 'Clinicians tuning out warnings because most flags are false, risking dismissal of true cases', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'The energy cost of running predictive models', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Patients becoming tired of being assessed', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'A model losing accuracy as data ages', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: 'When most flags are false, clinicians learn to tune them out — alert fatigue — which is dangerous because it can lead to dismissing the rare true signal the system exists to catch.' },
  { question: 'How does an algorithmic risk flag relate to the legal duty to protect?', options: [
    { text: 'The flag automatically triggers the duty and mandates protective action', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'The flag is upstream of the duty; a clinical determination of serious danger, not the algorithm, triggers or discharges it', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'The flag discharges the duty because the model evaluated the risk', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'The flag is legally irrelevant in every situation', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A low-precision flag is not a clinical judgment of serious or imminent danger. It can prompt assessment, and the resulting clinical determination may trigger duties — but the flag neither creates nor discharges the duty by itself.' },
  { question: 'A clinically generated suicide-risk score within a U.S. health system is:', options: [
    { text: 'Public information that may be shared freely', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Protected health information under HIPAA, requiring limited access and careful handling', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Exempt from all privacy law because it is algorithmically produced', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Required to be disclosed to the patient\'s employer', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A risk score generated from clinical data is protected health information under HIPAA, must be limited to those with a treatment, payment, or operations need, and should not become a permanent stigmatizing label.' },
  { question: 'Why is consent an acute ethical concern for social-media and device-based crisis detection?', options: [
    { text: 'Because users explicitly consent to clinical assessment when they post', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Because people generally did not consent to suicide-risk assessment, and broad terms of service are not informed consent for interventions with potential coercive consequences', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Because consent is never required for any health-related activity', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Because monitoring is always illegal', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'People who post, search, or carry devices generally did not consent to suicide-risk assessment; buried terms of service are not informed consent, particularly when detection can route a person to emergency services.' },
  { question: 'The equity concern in algorithmic risk scoring arises primarily because:', options: [
    { text: 'Algorithms are inherently unbiased and therefore always fair', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Models learn from historical data that encode inequities, potentially under- or over-flagging specific groups', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Equity is irrelevant to statistical prediction', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Models cannot be evaluated across demographic groups', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Because models learn from historical care patterns, they can under-flag under-assessed groups or over-flag over-surveilled groups, reproducing disparities under a veneer of objectivity. Performance should be evaluated separately across groups.' },
  { question: 'Structured professional judgment, applied to AI flags, is best characterized as:', options: [
    { text: 'Relying solely on the algorithm because it outperforms clinicians', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Relying solely on unstructured intuition', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Using empirically informed factors to organize the evaluation while the clinician retains final authority', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Delegating the decision to an organizational protocol', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: 'Structured professional judgment is the middle path: empirical inputs (including an AI flag) discipline the evaluation, while the clinician integrates context and makes the final, individualized determination.' },
  { question: 'The two predictable failure modes when integrating AI risk flags are:', options: [
    { text: 'Dismissal and over-reaction', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Calibration and discrimination', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Sensitivity and specificity', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Privacy and consent', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 0, explanation: 'Dismissal (clicking past flags through alert fatigue, forfeiting real signal) and over-reaction (coercive escalation on a low-precision flag) are the two failure modes; structured professional judgment steers between them.' },
  { question: 'For most algorithmic risk flags, the appropriate clinical destination is:', options: [
    { text: 'Immediate involuntary evaluation', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'A collaborative safety-planning conversation that includes 988 and means safety', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'No action, since most flags are false', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Forwarding the flag to the patient\'s family', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'Most flags warrant proportionate response: a collaborative, jointly authored safety plan that includes the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline and collaborative means-safety counseling, not emergency escalation.' },
  { question: 'The recommended safe pattern for crisis detection in consumer technology and clinical workflows is:', options: [
    { text: 'Let automated systems manage the crisis to completion', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Detect possible crisis signals, surface 988 and resources clearly, and route the person to trained human help', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Escalate every detection to emergency services without review', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Suppress crisis resources to avoid alarming users', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'The safe pattern is detect, surface 988 and resources, route to trained humans. Automation allocates attention and connects people to help; trained humans do the actual crisis work.' },
  { question: 'Why must conversational AI (chatbots) route suicidal disclosures rather than manage the crisis themselves?', options: [
    { text: 'Because chatbots are trained crisis counselors and prefer not to act', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Because language is ambiguous and a conversational system is not a crisis counselor; safe design pairs detection with routing to human help', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Because routing is legally prohibited', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Because chatbots cannot display the 988 number', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A chatbot is not a crisis counselor, and language detection is imperfect. The safe design recognizes possible crisis language, surfaces 988 clearly, and routes to human help rather than attempting to manage the crisis.' },
  { question: 'A responsible organizational crisis-detection protocol must include all of the following EXCEPT:', options: [
    { text: 'A defined human response specifying who is notified and what they do', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Automatic involuntary action triggered by any flag', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Explicit integration of the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Ongoing monitoring of performance, equity, and alert fatigue', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A defined human response, 988 integration, and ongoing monitoring are essential. Automatic involuntary action on any flag is the over-reaction failure mode at the organizational level and imposes disproportionate harm given the signal\'s low precision.' },
  { question: 'In the integrated case of Marcus, the coordinator\'s response to the flag exemplified:', options: [
    { text: 'Over-reaction through immediate involuntary evaluation', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'Dismissal of the flag as noise with no action', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'The prompt-not-verdict principle: outreach, structured assessment, and a proportionate, documented collaborative safety plan with 988', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'Forwarding the flag to emergency services without contact', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 2, explanation: 'The coordinator used the flag to prompt outreach and assessment, found passive ideation without imminent danger, and built a collaborative safety plan including 988 — avoiding both failure modes and anchoring the response in clinical findings.' },
  { question: 'The single best summary of how clinicians should treat algorithmic suicide-risk flags is:', options: [
    { text: 'As a verdict that replaces clinical assessment', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'As a low-precision prompt to conduct or update a competent, ethically grounded assessment, integrated into collaborative safety planning with 988', isCorrect: true },
    { text: 'As a reason to escalate every case to emergency services', isCorrect: false },
    { text: 'As statistical noise that can always be ignored', isCorrect: false }
  ], correctAnswer: 1, explanation: 'A flag is a low-precision, low-base-rate signal that prompts — never replaces — competent clinical assessment, integrated through structured professional judgment into collaborative safety planning referencing 988, with proportionality, equity, and quality assurance throughout.' }
];

